(window.LMT_ARTICLES = window.LMT_ARTICLES || {})["namemap"] = [{"id":"john-ivan-sean-yahya","icon":"👤","tags":["names","sound-change","borrowing","christianity","islam"],"title":{"en":"From Yoḥanan to John, Juan, Ivan, Seán and Yaḥyā","ja":"ヨーハーナーンからジョン、フアン、イヴァン、ショーン、ヤフヤーへ","ko":"요하난에서 존, 후안, 이반, 숀, 야흐야까지","zh":"从约哈难到约翰、胡安、伊万、肖恩和叶哈雅","yue":"由約哈難到約翰、胡安、伊凡、肖恩同葉哈雅","vi":"Từ Yoḥanan đến John, Juan, Ivan, Seán và Yaḥyā","th":"จากโยฮานันสู่จอห์น ฮวน อีวาน ชอน และยะห์ยา","id":"Dari Yoḥanan ke John, Juan, Ivan, Seán, dan Yaḥyā","hi":"योहानान से जॉन, हुआन, इवान, शॉन और याह्या तक","de":"Von Yoḥanan zu John, Juan, Ivan, Seán und Yaḥyā","fr":"De Yoḥanan à John, Juan, Ivan, Seán et Yaḥyā","it":"Da Yoḥanan a John, Juan, Ivan, Seán e Yaḥyā","es":"De Yoḥanan a John, Juan, Iván, Seán y Yaḥyā","pt":"De Yoḥanan a John, Juan, Ivan, Seán e Yaḥyā","ru":"От Йоханана к Джону, Хуану, Ивану, Шону и Яхье","uk":"Від Йоханана до Джона, Хуана, Івана, Шона та Ях'ї","ar":"من يوحانان إلى جون وخوان وإيفان وشون ويحيى","he":"מיוחנן לג'ון, חואן, איוואן, שון ויחיא","sw":"Kutoka Yoḥanan hadi John, Juan, Ivan, Seán na Yaḥyā"},"summary":{"en":"One Hebrew name fills 32 cells of the map. Greek dropped its ḥ, Romance languages each reshaped the Latin I-, Normans carried it to England and Ireland, Slavs got Ivan from Greek, and the Qur'an calls John the Baptist Yaḥyā.","ja":"ヘブライ語の一つの名前が、地図の32マスを埋めている。ギリシャ語で ḥ が落ち、ロマンス諸語はラテン語の語頭 I- をそれぞれ別の音に変えた。ノルマン人はこの名をイングランドとアイルランドへ運び、スラヴ人はギリシャ語からイヴァンを受け取った。コーランは洗礼者ヨハネをヤフヤーと呼ぶ。","ko":"히브리어 이름 하나가 지도의 32칸을 채운다. 그리스어는 ḥ를 버렸고, 로망스어들은 라틴어 I-를 저마다 다르게 바꾸었으며, 노르만인은 이 이름을 잉글랜드와 아일랜드로 가져갔고, 슬라브인은 그리스어에서 이반을 받아들였다. 그리고 쿠란은 세례자 요한을 야흐야라고 부른다.","zh":"一个希伯来名字占满了地图上的32个格子。希腊语丢掉了 ḥ，罗曼语各自改造了拉丁语的 I-，诺曼人把它带到英格兰和爱尔兰，斯拉夫人从希腊语得到了 Ivan，而《古兰经》称施洗约翰为叶哈雅（Yaḥyā）。","yue":"一個希伯來名，填滿咗地圖上32格。希臘語甩咗個 ḥ，羅曼語各自改造咗拉丁文嘅 I-，諾曼人將佢帶到英格蘭同愛爾蘭，斯拉夫人由希臘語攞到 Ivan，而《古蘭經》就叫施洗約翰做葉哈雅（Yaḥyā）。","vi":"Một cái tên Hebrew phủ kín 32 ô trên bản đồ. Tiếng Hy Lạp bỏ âm ḥ, các ngôn ngữ Rôman mỗi thứ biến đổi chữ I- của tiếng Latinh một kiểu, người Norman mang tên này sang Anh và Ireland, người Slav nhận Ivan từ tiếng Hy Lạp, còn kinh Qur'an gọi Gioan Tẩy Giả là Yaḥyā.","th":"ชื่อฮีบรูชื่อเดียวเติมเต็ม 32 ช่องบนแผนที่ ภาษากรีกทิ้งเสียง ḥ ภาษากลุ่มโรมานซ์ต่างดัดแปลง I- ของละตินไปคนละทาง ชาวนอร์มันนำชื่อนี้ไปอังกฤษและไอร์แลนด์ ชาวสลาฟได้ Ivan มาจากภาษากรีก และอัลกุรอานเรียกยอห์นผู้ให้บัพติศมาว่ายะห์ยา (Yaḥyā)","id":"Satu nama Ibrani mengisi 32 sel di peta. Bahasa Yunani membuang ḥ-nya, bahasa-bahasa Roman masing-masing membentuk ulang I- Latin, bangsa Norman membawanya ke Inggris dan Irlandia, bangsa Slavia mendapat Ivan dari bahasa Yunani, dan Al-Qur'an menyebut Yohanes Pembaptis Yaḥyā.","hi":"एक हिब्रू नाम मानचित्र के 32 ख़ाने भरता है। यूनानी ने उसका ḥ गिरा दिया, रोमांस भाषाओं ने लैटिन I- को अपने-अपने ढंग से ढाला, नॉर्मन उसे इंग्लैंड और आयरलैंड ले गए, स्लावों को इवान यूनानी से मिला, और क़ुरआन यूहन्ना बपतिस्मा देनेवाले को याह्या कहता है।","de":"Ein einziger hebräischer Name füllt 32 Zellen der Karte. Das Griechische ließ sein ḥ fallen, die romanischen Sprachen formten das lateinische I- jeweils auf eigene Weise um, die Normannen brachten den Namen nach England und Irland, die Slawen erhielten Ivan aus dem Griechischen, und der Koran nennt Johannes den Täufer Yaḥyā.","fr":"Un seul nom hébreu remplit 32 cases de la carte. Le grec a perdu son ḥ, chaque langue romane a remodelé à sa façon le I- latin, les Normands l’ont porté en Angleterre et en Irlande, les Slaves ont reçu Ivan du grec, et le Coran appelle Jean le Baptiste Yaḥyā.","it":"Un solo nome ebraico riempie 32 caselle della mappa. Il greco ha perso la sua ḥ, ogni lingua romanza ha rimodellato a modo suo la I- latina, i Normanni l’hanno portato in Inghilterra e in Irlanda, gli Slavi hanno ricevuto Ivan dal greco e il Corano chiama Giovanni Battista Yaḥyā.","es":"Un solo nombre hebreo llena 32 celdas del mapa. El griego perdió su ḥ, cada lengua romance remodeló a su manera la I- latina, los normandos lo llevaron a Inglaterra e Irlanda, los eslavos recibieron Iván del griego y el Corán llama Yaḥyā a Juan el Bautista.","pt":"Um único nome hebraico preenche 32 células do mapa. O grego perdeu o seu ḥ, cada língua românica remodelou à sua maneira o I- latino, os normandos o levaram para a Inglaterra e a Irlanda, os eslavos receberam Ivan do grego e o Alcorão chama João Batista de Yaḥyā.","ru":"Одно еврейское имя заполняет 32 ячейки карты. Греческий потерял ḥ, каждый романский язык по-своему переделал латинское I-, нормандцы донесли имя до Англии и Ирландии, славяне получили Ивана из греческого, а Коран называет Иоанна Крестителя Яхьей.","uk":"Одне єврейське ім'я заповнює 32 клітинки мапи. Грецька втратила ḥ, кожна романська мова по-своєму переробила латинське I-, нормани донесли ім'я до Англії та Ірландії, слов'яни отримали Івана з грецької, а Коран називає Івана Хрестителя Ях'єю.","ar":"اسم عبري واحد يملأ 32 خانة على الخريطة. أسقطت اليونانية حرف الحاء، وأعادت كل لغة رومانسية تشكيل الحرف اللاتيني I- على طريقتها، وحمله النورمان إلى إنجلترا وأيرلندا، وأخذ السلاف اسم إيفان عن اليونانية، ويسمّي القرآن يوحنا المعمدان يحيى.","he":"שם עברי אחד ממלא 32 משבצות במפה. היוונית השמיטה את ה־ḥ, כל אחת מהשפות הרומאניות עיצבה בדרכה את ה־I- הלטינית, הנורמנים הביאו את השם לאנגליה ולאירלנד, הסלאבים קיבלו את איוואן מהיוונית, והקוראן קורא ליוחנן המטביל יחיא.","sw":"Jina moja la Kiebrania linajaza visanduku 32 vya ramani. Kigiriki kiliacha ḥ yake, kila lugha ya Kiromansi ilibadilisha kwa namna yake I- ya Kilatini, Wanormani walilipeleka Uingereza na Ayalandi, Waslavi walipata Ivan kutoka Kigiriki, na Kurani inamwita Yohana Mbatizaji Yaḥyā."},"body":{"en":"<p>The Hebrew name <strong>Yōḥānān</strong> (יוֹחָנָן) joins <em>Yô-</em>, a short form of the divine name, to the verb <em>ḥānan</em> \"to be gracious\"; the Hebrew Bible also has the longer Yəhôḥānān (Jehohanan). On the map the John entry fills 32 of the 43 cells, and nearly every form goes back to one of two Gospel spellings: Greek <strong>Iōánnēs</strong> (Ἰωάννης) and Latin <strong>Iohannes</strong>.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"john\">👤 Show John on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"49\" data-lng=\"18\" data-zoom=\"4\">🌍 Pan to where Jan meets Ivan</button></div><h3>Greek drops the ḥ</h3><p>Greek had no pharyngeal <em>ḥ</em>, so Iōánnēs has none, and it took the masculine ending <em>-ēs</em> so that it could be declined like a Greek noun. Latin Iohannes writes an <em>h</em> between the vowels. Modern Greek keeps the old spelling, Ιωάννης, but says <em>Ioánnis</em> /i.oˈa.nis/, because <em>ē</em> has become <em>i</em>; the everyday form is Giannis, as in Giannis Antetokounmpo. Georgian <em>Ioane</em> and Romanian <em>Ioan</em> stay close to the church form; in Romania the everyday form is Ion, as in the writer Ion Creangă.</p><h3>One Latin letter, three Romance sounds</h3><p>Latin <em>I</em> before a vowel was the consonant /j/. Across most of the Romance-speaking area it hardened to /dʒ/: Latin <em>iocus</em> \"jest\" became Italian <em>gioco</em>, French <em>jeu</em> and Spanish <em>juego</em>. Italian kept /dʒ/ and spells it <em>gi-</em>: <strong>Giovanni</strong> /dʒoˈvanni/, with a <em>v</em> filling the gap between <em>o</em> and <em>a</em>. Old French <em>Jehan</em> also began with /dʒ/; in the 13th century French reduced /dʒ/ to /ʒ/, which gives <strong>Jean</strong> /ʒɑ̃/. Portuguese has the same /ʒ/ and turned the final <em>-an</em> into a nasal diphthong: <strong>João</strong> /ʒwɐ̃w/. Old Spanish pronounced the <em>j</em> of Juan as /ʒ/ as well, but in the 16th and early 17th centuries Spanish devoiced it to /ʃ/ and then moved it back to the velar /x/, so <strong>Juan</strong> is now /xwan/. Spanish took the name to the Philippines, where Filipino Juan /hwan/ is the everyman \"Juan dela Cruz\".</p><h3>John and Seán: Norman imports</h3><p>English took the name from Norman French. It did so before French lost the stop in /dʒ/, so <strong>John</strong> /dʒɒn/ keeps the older French consonant, as <em>joy</em> and <em>judge</em> do; the <em>h</em> in the spelling comes from Latin <em>Johannes</em>. The Anglo-Normans who arrived in Ireland from 1169 brought <em>Jehan</em> with them. Irish had no /dʒ/ or /ʒ/ at the start of a word and used a slender <em>s</em>, /ʃ/, in its place: older <em>Seaghán</em>, now <strong>Seán</strong> /ʃaːn/. In <em>Irish Names and Surnames</em> (1923), Patrick Woulfe calls it \"one of the commonest names among the early Anglo-Norman settlers\" and lists it as a variant of <em>Eoin</em>, the older Irish form taken from Latin.</p><h3>Where Latin j stayed j</h3><p>German, Scandinavian, Dutch, West Slavic, Hungarian and Finnish speakers read Latin <em>J</em> as /j/, so the consonant never changed, and the forms differ in how much of the word is left. German has <strong>Johannes</strong> /joˈhanəs/, shortened to Johann (Johann Sebastian Bach) and, with the first syllable gone, to Hans. Swedish and Norwegian have <em>Johan</em>, Finnish <em>Juhani</em>, and Hungarian <em>János</em>, which keeps the Latin <em>-s</em>. Dutch, Polish and Czech cut it down to one syllable, <strong>Jan</strong>: Jan Steen, Jan Hus, Jan III Sobieski. Missionary forms further away follow the same pattern: Indonesian <em>Yohanes</em>, Korean 요한 <em>Yohan</em>, and Vietnamese Catholic <em>Gioan</em>, spelled with <em>Gi-</em> and pronounced /z/ in Hanoi and /j/ in the south.</p><h3>Ivan: the Greek line</h3><p>The Slavs converted from Constantinople got the name from Greek through Church Slavonic, which wrote Иоаннъ (<em>Ioannŭ</em>). In everyday speech it became <strong>Ivan</strong>: the <em>o</em> between <em>i</em> and <em>a</em> turned into <em>v</em>, where Italian Giovanni kept the <em>o</em> and added a <em>v</em> after it. Russian Иван /ɪˈvan/ and Ukrainian Іван /iˈʋɑn/ show it. Croatian also has Ivan, although Croatia is Catholic and its Catholic neighbours to the north, Poland and Czechia, say Jan. The line between the two forms does not follow church allegiance exactly.</p><h3>Forms that keep the guttural</h3><p>Several forms to the east of Greece keep the middle consonant that Greek lost. Arabic-speaking Christians, among them the Copts of Egypt (the map's Arabic cell), say <strong>Yūḥannā</strong> (يوحنا), with <em>ḥ</em>, like Syriac <em>Yuḥanan</em>. Amharic <strong>ዮሐንስ</strong> <em>Yoḥannis</em>, the name of Emperor Yohannes IV, is spelled with the Ge'ez letter ሐ <em>ḥa</em>. Armenian <strong>Hovhannes</strong> has an <em>h</em> in the middle, and its first <em>H</em> comes from the classical spelling Յովհաննէս, whose letter յ is pronounced <em>h</em> at the start of a word. Modern Hebrew pronounces the original יוֹחָנָן as <em>Yoḥanan</em> /joχaˈnan/.</p><h3>Yaḥyā</h3><p>The Qur'an calls John the Baptist <strong>Yaḥyā</strong> (يحيى) five times (3:39, 6:85, 19:7, 19:12, 21:90), and 19:7 says that the name had not been given to anyone before him. How Yaḥyā relates to Yōḥānān is disputed. Muslim tradition reads it as the Arabic verb form \"he lives\", from the root <em>ḥ-y-y</em>. Some Western scholars have proposed instead that it comes from a misreading of a form like Yuḥannā written without dots. A tomb inscription from al-Ula in northwestern Arabia, dated to year 201 of the local era (AD 306/307), was set up by a <em>Yḥyʾ</em> son of Simon for his father Simon; it appears in Robert Hoyland's 2011 study of the Jewish inscriptions of the Hijaz. So the name was in use in a Jewish family three centuries before the Qur'an. On the map, the Persian cell shows Yahyā (یحیی). In Indonesia the Christian form is Yohanes and the Muslim form is Yahya.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"Campbell, Mike. \"John\" (and \"Sean\"). Behind the Name, accessed 2026.","url":"https://www.behindthename.com/name/john"},{"text":"Woulfe, Patrick (1923). Sloinnte Gaedheal is Gall: Irish Names and Surnames. Dublin: M. H. Gill & Son. Entry Seaghán/Seán (Library Ireland edition).","url":"https://libraryireland.com/names/men/sean-sean.php"},{"text":"Penny, Ralph (2002). A History of the Spanish Language, 2nd ed. Cambridge University Press. (Devoicing of /ʒ/ to /ʃ/ and its retraction to /x/ in the 16th and 17th centuries.)"},{"text":"Hoyland, Robert G. (2011). \"The Jews of the Hijaz in the Qurʾān and in their inscriptions.\" In G. S. Reynolds (ed.), New Perspectives on the Qurʾān, Routledge, pp. 91–116. (al-Ula tomb inscription of Yḥyʾ son of Simon, year 201 = AD 306/307; the two readings of the name are summarised in Wiktionary's entry يحيى.)","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%D9%8A%D8%AD%D9%8A%D9%89"}]},{"id":"james-jacob-santiago","icon":"🐚","tags":["biblical-names","doublets","latin","spanish","onomastics"],"title":{"en":"James, Jacob and Santiago: One Hebrew Name","ja":"ジェームズもヤコブもサンティアゴも——一つのヘブライ語名","ko":"제임스, 야곱, 산티아고: 하나의 히브리어 이름","zh":"詹姆斯、雅各与圣地亚哥：同一个希伯来名字","yue":"詹姆斯、雅各同聖地亞哥：原來係同一個希伯來名","vi":"James, Jacob và Santiago: chung một cái tên Hebrew","th":"เจมส์ ยาโคบ และซันติอาโก: ชื่อฮีบรูชื่อเดียวกัน","id":"James, Jacob, dan Santiago: Satu Nama Ibrani","hi":"जेम्स, जैकब और सांतियागो: एक ही हिब्रू नाम","de":"James, Jacob und Santiago: ein hebräischer Name","fr":"James, Jacob et Santiago : un seul nom hébreu","it":"James, Jacob e Santiago: un solo nome ebraico","es":"James, Jacob y Santiago: un mismo nombre hebreo","pt":"James, Jacob e Santiago: um só nome hebraico","ru":"Джеймс, Иаков и Сантьяго: одно древнееврейское имя","uk":"Джеймс, Яків і Сантьяго: одне давньоєврейське ім'я","ar":"جيمس ويعقوب وسانتياغو: اسم عبري واحد","he":"ג'יימס, יעקב וסנטיאגו: שם עברי אחד","sw":"James, Jacob na Santiago: Jina Moja la Kiebrania"},"summary":{"en":"Jacob, James, Jacques, Giacomo, Santiago and Tiago all go back to Hebrew Yaʿaqov. A Greek grammatical choice and a spoken-Latin variant split them apart. Diego, long counted among them, probably comes from another name.","ja":"Jacob、James、Jacques、Giacomo、Santiago、Tiago はどれもヘブライ語のヤアコブにさかのぼる。分かれたきっかけは、ギリシャ語訳での扱いの違いと、話し言葉のラテン語に生まれた異形だった。長く同じ仲間とされてきたディエゴは、別の名から来た可能性が高い。","ko":"Jacob, James, Jacques, Giacomo, Santiago, Tiago는 모두 히브리어 Yaʿaqov로 거슬러 올라간다. 그리스어의 문법적 선택 하나와 구어 라틴어의 변이형 하나가 이들을 갈라놓았다. 오랫동안 같은 무리로 여겨진 Diego는 아마 다른 이름에서 왔을 것이다.","zh":"Jacob、James、Jacques、Giacomo、Santiago 和 Tiago 都可追溯到希伯来语的 Yaʿaqov。希腊语的一个语法选择和通俗拉丁语的一个变体，让它们分道扬镳。长期被归入其中的 Diego，很可能出自另一个名字。","yue":"Jacob、James、Jacques、Giacomo、Santiago 同 Tiago，全部都源自希伯來文嘅 Yaʿaqov。希臘文一個文法上嘅取捨，加上口語拉丁文一個變體，令佢哋分咗家。長久以來被當成同一家嘅 Diego，好可能係出自另一個名。","vi":"Jacob, James, Jacques, Giacomo, Santiago và Tiago đều bắt nguồn từ tên Hebrew Yaʿaqov. Một lựa chọn ngữ pháp trong tiếng Hy Lạp và một biến thể của tiếng Latinh khẩu ngữ đã tách chúng ra. Diego, vốn lâu nay được xếp chung nhóm, có lẽ lại xuất phát từ một cái tên khác.","th":"Jacob, James, Jacques, Giacomo, Santiago และ Tiago ล้วนสืบย้อนไปถึงชื่อภาษาฮีบรู Yaʿaqov การเลือกทางไวยากรณ์ครั้งหนึ่งในภาษากรีกกับรูปแปรหนึ่งของภาษาละตินภาษาพูดทำให้ชื่อเหล่านี้แยกทางกัน ส่วน Diego ที่เคยถูกนับรวมอยู่ในกลุ่มนี้มานาน น่าจะมาจากอีกชื่อหนึ่ง","id":"Jacob, James, Jacques, Giacomo, Santiago, dan Tiago semuanya berasal dari nama Ibrani Yaʿaqov. Sebuah pilihan tata bahasa dalam bahasa Yunani dan sebuah varian bahasa Latin lisan memisahkan mereka. Diego, yang lama dianggap termasuk kelompok ini, kemungkinan besar berasal dari nama lain.","hi":"जैकब, जेम्स, ज़ाक, जाकोमो, सांतियागो और तियागो, सबकी जड़ हिब्रू नाम याअकोव में है। यूनानी के एक व्याकरणिक चुनाव और बोलचाल की लैटिन के एक रूपांतर ने इन्हें अलग-अलग राहों पर डाल दिया। दिएगो को लंबे समय तक इसी परिवार में गिना जाता रहा, पर संभवतः वह किसी दूसरे नाम से आया है।","de":"Jacob, James, Jacques, Giacomo, Santiago und Tiago gehen alle auf das hebräische Yaʿaqov zurück. Eine grammatische Entscheidung im Griechischen und eine Variante des gesprochenen Lateins haben sie auseinandergeführt. Diego, lange zu ihnen gezählt, stammt wahrscheinlich von einem anderen Namen.","fr":"Jacob, James, Jacques, Giacomo, Santiago et Tiago remontent tous à l'hébreu Yaʿaqov. Un choix grammatical du grec et une variante du latin parlé les ont séparés. Diego, longtemps compté parmi eux, vient probablement d'un autre nom.","it":"Jacob, James, Jacques, Giacomo, Santiago e Tiago risalgono tutti all'ebraico Yaʿaqov. Una scelta grammaticale del greco e una variante del latino parlato li hanno separati. Diego, a lungo annoverato tra loro, viene probabilmente da un altro nome.","es":"Jacob, James, Jacques, Giacomo, Santiago y Tiago se remontan todos al hebreo Yaʿaqov. Una elección gramatical del griego y una variante del latín hablado los separaron. Diego, contado durante mucho tiempo entre ellos, probablemente procede de otro nombre.","pt":"Jacob, James, Jacques, Giacomo, Santiago e Tiago remontam todos ao hebraico Yaʿaqov. Uma escolha gramatical do grego e uma variante do latim falado os separaram. Diego, por muito tempo contado entre eles, provavelmente vem de outro nome.","ru":"Джейкоб, Джеймс, Жак, Джакомо, Сантьяго и Тиаго восходят к древнееврейскому Яаков. Развели их выбор греческой грамматической формы и вариант, возникший в народной латыни. Диего, которого долго причисляли к этому ряду, скорее всего, происходит от другого имени.","uk":"Джейкоб, Джеймс, Жак, Джакомо, Сантьяго й Тіаго походять від давньоєврейського Яаков. Розвели їх вибір грецької граматичної форми та варіант, що виник у народній латині. Дієго, якого довго зараховували до цього ряду, найімовірніше, походить від іншого імені.","ar":"تعود الأسماء جيكوب وجيمس وجاك وجاكومو وسانتياغو وتياغو كلها إلى الاسم العبري يَعَقوف. وقد فرّق بينها اختيارٌ نحوي في اليونانية وصيغةٌ نشأت في اللاتينية المحكية. أما دييغو، الذي عُدّ منها زمنًا طويلًا، فالأرجح أنه يعود إلى اسم آخر.","he":"ג'ייקוב, ג'יימס, ז'אק, ג'קומו, סנטיאגו וטיאגו – כולם חוזרים אל השם העברי יעקב. בחירה דקדוקית ביוונית וגרסה של הלטינית המדוברת הפרידו ביניהם. דייגו, שנמנה עליהם זמן רב, בא כנראה משם אחר.","sw":"Jacob, James, Jacques, Giacomo, Santiago na Tiago yote yanatokana na jina la Kiebrania Yaʿaqov. Uamuzi mmoja wa kisarufi katika Kigiriki na umbo mbadala la Kilatini cha mazungumzo viliyatenganisha. Diego, ambalo kwa muda mrefu lilihesabiwa miongoni mwao, huenda linatokana na jina jingine."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A heel, or a prayer</h3><p>The Hebrew name is <strong>Yaʿaqov</strong> (יַעֲקֹב). Genesis 25:26 explains it with a pun: the second twin was born holding his brother Esau's heel, <em>ʿaqev</em>. Commentators usually take it instead as the shortened form of a longer name of the type <em>Yaʿqub-ʾel</em>, \"may El protect\". Gordon Wenham's 1994 commentary on Genesis calls this a typical Amorite name of the early second millennium BCE and cites examples from Chagar Bazar in Syria, around 1800 BCE. Even in that reconstruction, the meaning of the verb is uncertain.</p><h3>Jacob for the patriarch, James for the apostles</h3><p>The Greek translators treated the name in two ways. The Septuagint and the New Testament leave the patriarch as <em>Iakōb</em> (Ἰακώβ), a form that never changes its ending. The two apostles called James, and the author of the Epistle of James, get <em>Iakōbos</em> (Ἰάκωβος), which takes Greek case endings. Jerome's Latin Vulgate keeps the same split, with <em>Iacob</em> and <em>Iacobus</em>. English Bibles made these into two separate names, Jacob and James, which is why the letter is the Epistle of James in English. Korean Protestant Bibles also use two forms, 야곱 <em>Yagop</em> and 야고보 <em>Yagobo</em>. Japanese uses ヤコブ for both, and Chinese uses 雅各 for both.</p><h3>Iacomus and Iacobus</h3><p>Spoken Latin produced a variant, <strong>Iacomus</strong>, with <em>m</em> in place of <em>b</em>. It is the source of Old French <em>James</em>, which English borrowed. It also gave Italian <strong>Giacomo</strong> (Giacomo Puccini on the map), Catalan <em>Jaume</em>, and Spanish and Portuguese <em>Jaime</em>. The unchanged <em>Iacobus</em> gave French <strong>Jacques</strong>, German, Dutch and Swedish <strong>Jakob</strong>, Polish and Czech <strong>Jakub</strong>, and Hungarian <strong>Jakab</strong>. Russian <strong>Яков</strong> and Ukrainian <strong>Яків</strong> came straight from the Greek through Church Slavonic. English kept the Latin <em>b</em> in derived words. The <em>Jacobean</em> period is the reign of James I (1603–1625), and the <em>Jacobites</em> supported the exiled James II and his heirs after 1688.</p><h3>Sant Iago</h3><p>In medieval Spanish, <em>Iacobus</em> wore down to <em>Iago</em> or <em>Yago</em>, so the apostle James, son of Zebedee, became <em>Sant Iago</em>, \"Saint James\". The Compostela legend says that around 814 a hermit named Pelagius found his tomb in Galicia. Bishop Theodemir of Iria Flavia then reported it to Alfonso II of Asturias (r. 791–842). The pilgrim roads to the city that grew up there are the Camino de Santiago. The saint's title fused into a single given name, <strong>Santiago</strong>. That is the form in the Spain, Mexico and Philippines cells, and the Spain cell lists Santiago Ramón y Cajal. Portuguese divided the word in a different place, reading it as <em>São Tiago</em>, and took <strong>Tiago</strong> as the name. Brazilian <strong>Thiago</strong>, as in the footballer Thiago Silva, keeps the <em>th</em> that Portugal's 1911 spelling reform dropped from ordinary words. Welsh <em>Iago</em> is the same name, and Shakespeare gave it to the villain of <em>Othello</em>.</p><h3>Diego, the disputed member</h3><p>Robert Southey's 1808 <em>Chronicle of the Cid</em> treated <strong>Diego</strong> as a variant of Tiago, the shortened Santiago. In 1891 the Asturian lexicographer Apolinar Rato y Hevia listed Diego with Yago, Tiago and Jacobo as forms of Santiago. The medieval records point elsewhere. Latin <em>Didacus</em> appears in Iberian documents from 747, the date Antonio Floriano gave in 1949 for its oldest record. It is written <em>Diaco</em> and <em>Diago</em> in the 10th century. <em>Diego</em> itself first appears in the late 11th century and becomes the standard spelling in the 14th. The saint known in Spanish as San Diego de Alcalá (d. 1463) is <em>Didacus</em> in Latin, and Sebastián Vizcaíno named the bay of San Diego after him in 1602. Yakov Malkiel had already argued against equating Diego with Santiago in 1975. Two questions remain open. Nobody knows where Didacus itself comes from; Lidia Becker's 2009 survey of Iberian personal names from the 6th to 12th centuries rejects a Greek origin in <em>Diadochus</em>, as well as Basque and Celtic proposals. Becker also notes that the change from <em>Diago</em> to <em>Diego</em> has no agreed explanation. The surname <em>Díaz</em> means \"son of Diego\".</p><h3>Yaʿqūb</h3><p>The Qurʾan calls the prophet <strong>Yaʿqūb</strong> (يعقوب). This line comes from the Semitic form directly, not through Greek. It continues in Persian and Urdu یعقوب, Turkish <strong>Yakup</strong>, Indonesian <strong>Yakub</strong> and Hausa <strong>Yakubu</strong>, as in Yakubu Gowon, Nigeria's head of state from 1966 to 1975. Chinese Muslim usage, including Ma Jian's translation of the Qurʾan, writes the name 叶尔孤白 <em>Yèʼěrgūbái</em>, and that is the form in the China cell. The name appears in 31 of the map's 43 cells. The two English-language cells have one doublet each: <strong>Jacob</strong> for the United States and <strong>James</strong> for the United Kingdom.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"jacob\">👤 Show Jacob on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"42.88\" data-lng=\"-8.54\" data-zoom=\"5\">🌍 Pan to Santiago de Compostela</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"Becker, Lidia (2009). Hispano-romanisches Namenbuch: Untersuchung der Personennamen vorrömischer, griechischer und lateinisch-romanischer Etymologie auf der Iberischen Halbinsel im Mittelalter (6.–12. Jahrhundert). Patronymica Romanica 23. Tübingen: Niemeyer / De Gruyter (entry Didacus/Diego).","url":"https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/9783484971264/html"},{"text":"Wenham, Gordon J. (1994). Genesis 16–50. Word Biblical Commentary 2. Dallas: Word Books (on Yaʿaqov as a short form of Yaʿqub-ʾel, a typical early-2nd-millennium Amorite name; Chagar Bazar attestation c. 1800 BC)."},{"text":"Wikipedia (2026). Diego — Onomatology section (Tiago vs. Didacus hypotheses; Southey 1808, Rato y Hevia 1891, Floriano 1949, Malkiel 1975, Becker 2009).","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diego"},{"text":"Wikipedia (2026). Santiago de Compostela Cathedral (tomb legend: Pelagius, 814; Bishop Theodemir of Iria Flavia; Alfonso II, r. 791–842).","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santiago_de_Compostela_Cathedral"}]},{"id":"mary-maryam-miriam","icon":"🌹","tags":["etymology","religion","given-names","greek","spanish"],"title":{"en":"Mary, Maryam, Miriam: One Name, Two Endings","ja":"マリア、マルヤム、ミリアム——一つの名と二つの語尾","ko":"마리아, 마리암, 미리암: 하나의 이름, 두 가지 끝소리","zh":"玛利亚、麦尔彦、米利暗：一个名字，两种结尾","yue":"瑪利亞、麥爾彥、米利暗：一個名，兩種收尾","vi":"Maria, Maryam, Miriam: một cái tên, hai kiểu kết thúc","th":"มารีย์ มัรยัม มีเรียม: ชื่อเดียว สองแบบลงท้าย","id":"Maria, Maryam, Miriam: Satu Nama, Dua Akhiran","hi":"मैरी, मरियम, मिरियम: एक नाम, दो अंत","de":"Maria, Maryam, Mirjam: Ein Name, zwei Endungen","fr":"Marie, Maryam, Myriam : un nom, deux terminaisons","it":"Maria, Maryam, Miriam: un nome, due finali","es":"María, Maryam, Miriam: un nombre, dos finales","pt":"Maria, Maryam, Miriam: um nome, duas terminações","ru":"Мария, Марьям, Мириам: одно имя, два окончания","uk":"Марія, Мар'ям, Мір'ям: одне ім'я, два закінчення","ar":"ماري ومريم وميريام: اسم واحد ونهايتان","he":"מרי, מריאם, מרים: שם אחד, שתי סיומות","sw":"Maria, Maryam, Miriamu: Jina Moja, Miisho Miwili"},"summary":{"en":"On the map the name of Moses' sister and Jesus' mother splits into forms ending in -m and forms ending in a vowel, a fork already present in the Greek Bible. Its meaning is unknown, Irish keeps one form for the Virgin alone, and Spanish turns her titles into women's names.","ja":"モーセの姉、そしてイエスの母の名は、地図の上で -m で終わる形と母音で終わる形に分かれる。この分かれ目はギリシャ語訳聖書の時点ですでにある。名前の意味はわかっていない。アイルランド語には聖母だけに使う形があり、スペイン語では聖母の称号がそのまま女性の名になった。","ko":"모세의 누이와 예수의 어머니가 지닌 이 이름은 지도에서 -m으로 끝나는 형태와 모음으로 끝나는 형태로 갈리며, 이 갈림은 그리스어 성경에 이미 나타납니다. 이름의 뜻은 알려져 있지 않고, 아일랜드어는 한 형태를 성모에게만 쓰며, 스페인어는 성모의 칭호를 여성 이름으로 바꿉니다.","zh":"摩西的姐姐和耶稣的母亲共有的这个名字，在地图上分成以 -m 结尾和以元音结尾的两类，这一分岔在希腊文圣经中就已出现。它的含义无人知晓；爱尔兰语有一种形式只留给圣母，西班牙语则把圣母的称号变成了女子的名字。","yue":"摩西嘅家姐同耶穌嘅母親用嘅呢個名，喺地圖上分成以 -m 收尾同以元音收尾兩派，呢個分岔喺希臘文聖經已經有。個名嘅意思冇人知；愛爾蘭文有一個形式淨係留畀聖母用，西班牙文就將聖母嘅稱號變成女仔名。","vi":"Trên bản đồ, cái tên chung của chị Môsê và mẹ Đức Giêsu tách thành các dạng tận cùng bằng -m và các dạng tận cùng bằng nguyên âm, một chỗ rẽ đã có sẵn trong Kinh Thánh tiếng Hy Lạp. Không ai biết tên này nghĩa là gì; tiếng Ireland giữ riêng một dạng cho Đức Trinh Nữ, còn tiếng Tây Ban Nha biến các tước hiệu của Đức Mẹ thành tên phụ nữ.","th":"บนแผนที่ ชื่อของพี่สาวโมเสสและมารดาของพระเยซูแยกออกเป็นรูปที่ลงท้ายด้วย -m และรูปที่ลงท้ายด้วยสระ ทางแยกนี้มีอยู่แล้วในคัมภีร์ไบเบิลภาษากรีก ไม่มีใครรู้ความหมายของชื่อนี้ ภาษาไอริชสงวนรูปหนึ่งไว้สำหรับพระนางพรหมจารีเท่านั้น ส่วนภาษาสเปนนำสมญาของพระนางมาเป็นชื่อผู้หญิง","id":"Di peta, nama saudari Musa dan ibu Yesus terbelah menjadi bentuk yang berakhiran -m dan bentuk yang berakhiran vokal, percabangan yang sudah ada dalam Alkitab Yunani. Artinya tidak diketahui, bahasa Irlandia menyimpan satu bentuk khusus untuk Sang Perawan, dan bahasa Spanyol menjadikan gelar-gelarnya nama perempuan.","hi":"नक्शे पर मूसा की बहन और यीशु की माँ का नाम दो हिस्सों में बँट जाता है: -m पर ख़त्म होने वाले रूप और स्वर पर ख़त्म होने वाले रूप। यह बँटवारा यूनानी बाइबिल में ही मौजूद है। नाम का अर्थ अज्ञात है, आयरिश एक रूप केवल कुँवारी मरियम के लिए रखती है, और स्पेनिश उनकी उपाधियों को स्त्रियों के नाम बना देती है।","de":"Auf der Karte spaltet sich der Name der Schwester des Mose und der Mutter Jesu in Formen auf -m und Formen auf einen Vokal – eine Gabelung, die schon in der griechischen Bibel angelegt ist. Seine Bedeutung ist unbekannt, das Irische behält eine Form allein der Jungfrau vor, und im Spanischen werden ihre Titel zu Frauennamen.","fr":"Sur la carte, le nom de la sœur de Moïse et de la mère de Jésus se partage entre des formes en -m et des formes en voyelle, une bifurcation déjà présente dans la Bible grecque. Son sens reste inconnu, l'irlandais réserve une forme à la seule Vierge, et l'espagnol fait de ses titres des prénoms féminins.","it":"Sulla mappa il nome della sorella di Mosè e della madre di Gesù si divide in forme che terminano in -m e forme che terminano in vocale, una biforcazione già presente nella Bibbia greca. Il suo significato è sconosciuto, l'irlandese riserva una forma alla sola Vergine e lo spagnolo trasforma i suoi titoli in nomi femminili.","es":"En el mapa, el nombre de la hermana de Moisés y de la madre de Jesús se reparte entre formas que terminan en -m y formas que terminan en vocal, una bifurcación que ya aparece en la Biblia griega. Su significado se desconoce, el irlandés reserva una forma solo para la Virgen y el español convierte sus advocaciones en nombres de mujer.","pt":"No mapa, o nome da irmã de Moisés e da mãe de Jesus se divide entre formas terminadas em -m e formas terminadas em vogal, uma bifurcação que já existe na Bíblia grega. Seu significado é desconhecido, o irlandês guarda uma forma só para a Virgem e o espanhol transforma os títulos dela em nomes de mulher.","ru":"На карте имя сестры Моисея и матери Иисуса распадается на формы с окончанием на -m и формы с окончанием на гласный — развилка, которая есть уже в греческой Библии. Значение имени неизвестно, ирландский хранит одну форму только для Девы Марии, а испанский превращает её титулы в женские имена.","uk":"На мапі ім'я сестри Мойсея і матері Ісуса розпадається на форми із закінченням на -m і форми із закінченням на голосний — розвилка, яка є вже в грецькій Біблії. Значення імені невідоме, ірландська зберігає одну форму лише для Діви Марії, а іспанська перетворює її титули на жіночі імена.","ar":"على الخريطة ينقسم اسم أخت موسى وأم يسوع إلى صيغ تنتهي بـ -m وأخرى تنتهي بحرف علة، وهو انقسام موجود أصلًا في الكتاب المقدس اليوناني. معنى الاسم مجهول، والأيرلندية تحتفظ بصيغة منه للعذراء وحدها، والإسبانية تحوّل ألقابها إلى أسماء نسائية.","he":"במפה מתפצל שמן של אחות משה ושל אם ישו לצורות המסתיימות ב־m- ולצורות המסתיימות בתנועה, פיצול שקיים כבר בכתבי הקודש ביוונית. פירוש השם אינו ידוע, האירית שומרת צורה אחת לבתולה בלבד, והספרדית הופכת את תאריה לשמות נשים.","sw":"Kwenye ramani, jina la dada yake Musa na mama yake Yesu linagawanyika kati ya maumbo yanayoishia kwa -m na yale yanayoishia kwa irabu, mgawanyiko ambao tayari upo katika Biblia ya Kigiriki. Maana yake haijulikani, Kiayalandi kinatumia umbo moja kwa Bikira peke yake, na Kihispania kinageuza majina yake ya heshima kuwa majina ya wanawake."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Two endings on one map</h3><p>Select <strong>Mary</strong> on the map and the cells split by their last sound. Hebrew מִרְיָם <em>Miryam</em>, Arabic مريم and Persian مریم <em>Maryam</em>, Urdu مریم <em>Maryam</em>, Turkish <em>Meryem</em> and Amharic ማርያም <em>Maryam</em> end in <em>-m</em>, and Swahili <em>Mariamu</em> keeps the <em>m</em> and adds a final <em>-u</em>. Everywhere else the name ends in a vowel: Spanish <em>María</em>, Italian, German, Polish and Portuguese <em>Maria</em>, Greek <em>Μαρία</em>, Russian <em>Мария</em>, French <em>Marie</em>, English <em>Mary</em>.</p><p>The fork is already there in the Greek Bible. The Septuagint, whose translation began in the 3rd century BCE, spells Moses' sister Μαριαμ <em>Mariam</em> (Exodus 15:20). The New Testament uses both <em>Mariam</em> and <em>Maria</em> for the mother of Jesus. The first carries the Semitic name over unchanged and takes no case endings. The second is a Greek feminine noun in <em>-a</em>, so it could take Greek case endings. Latin took <em>Maria</em>, and so did the languages of Latin Christianity. The <em>-m</em> form went on into Arabic. The 19th sura of the Qurʼan is titled <em>Maryam</em>, and she is the only woman the Qurʼan names. The Amharic cell shows that the split is not Christian against Muslim: <em>Maryam</em> is also the Virgin's name in the Ethiopian Orthodox Church.</p><p>The vowels have a history too. The <em>i</em> of Hebrew <em>Miryam</em> comes from the Masoretic vowel marks, which were added to the text between the 7th and 10th centuries CE. The Greek <em>Mariam</em> is many centuries older, and it has an <em>a</em> there.</p><h3>What the name meant</h3><p>Nobody knows. In 1895 Otto Bardenhewer published a monograph, <em>Der Name Maria</em>, that discussed about seventy proposed meanings. The main ones are these:</p><ul><li><strong>Egyptian <em>mr</em> \"love\" or <em>mry</em> \"beloved\"</strong>, the element in names such as Meritamun, \"beloved of Amun\". Behind the Name calls an Egyptian origin the most likely. Anthony Maas, in the 1912 <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, judged that \"such a derivation is only possible, or at best barely probable.\"</li><li><strong>Hebrew <em>mar</em> \"bitter\"</strong>, the same root as <em>myrrh</em>. Patrick Woulfe's <em>Irish Names and Surnames</em> (1923) prefers this one and reads it as bitterness in the sense of grief.</li><li><strong>Hebrew <em>meri</em> \"rebellion\"</strong>, the connection given in Strong's Concordance (1890).</li><li><strong>Jerome's \"drop of the sea\"</strong>, Latin <em>stilla maris</em>, which reads <em>mar</em> as \"drop\" and <em>yam</em> as \"sea\". Later copies changed it to <em>stella maris</em>, \"star of the sea\". One Bamberg manuscript from the end of the 9th century still has <em>stilla</em>. \"Star of the Sea\" became a title of the Virgin, as in the hymn <em>Ave maris stella</em>.</li></ul><p>Behind the Name and baby-name books also list \"wished-for child\", but they give no derivation for it.</p><h3>A form kept for one woman</h3><p>Irish has two forms of the name. Women are called <em>Máire</em>. <em>Muire</em> is used only for the Virgin. The greeting <em>Dia dhuit</em>, \"God to you\", is answered with <em>Dia is Muire dhuit</em>, \"God and Mary to you\". The one known exception is the song <em>Caoineadh na dTrí Mhuire</em>, the lament of the three Marys at the crucifixion. Woulfe found only a few Irishwomen named Mary before the 17th century. Polish makes a similar distinction: in prayer the Virgin is <em>Maryja</em>, while a girl is named <em>Maria</em>, the form shown on the map.</p><h3>María for men, and the Virgin's titles as names</h3><p>In a Spanish double name, the first part sets the gender. <em>José María</em> is a man, like the former prime minister José María Aznar. <em>María José</em> is a woman. María also follows other men's names, as in Carlos María, Juan María and Luis María. Men outside Spain carry it too: Carl Maria von Weber, Rainer Maria Rilke, Jean-Marie Le Pen.</p><p>Many Spanish women's names are titles of the Virgin with the María taken off:</p><ul><li><strong>Dolores</strong> comes from <em>Nuestra Señora de los Dolores</em>, Our Lady of Sorrows.</li><li><strong>Pilar</strong> comes from the Virgen del Pilar of Zaragoza, who by tradition appeared to Saint James on a pillar.</li><li><strong>Rosario</strong> comes from the Rosary.</li><li><strong>Mercedes</strong> comes from <em>María de las Mercedes</em>, Mary of the Mercies.</li><li><strong>Carmen</strong> comes from Our Lady of Mount Carmel, from Hebrew <em>karmel</em> \"garden, orchard\". Its spelling was later bent toward Latin <em>carmen</em>, \"song\".</li></ul><p>In the population count the longer form still comes first. On 1 January 2024, Spain's National Statistics Institute (INE) counted 624,368 women named María Carmen, which made it the most frequent woman's name in Spain. There were 551,105 women named María and 340,920 named Carmen.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"mary\">👤 Show Mary on the map</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"Maas, Anthony (1912). \"The Name of Mary.\" The Catholic Encyclopedia, vol. 15. Robert Appleton Company (cites Bardenhewer, Der Name Maria, 1895; Jerome's stilla maris; Bamberg manuscript).","url":"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/15464a.htm"},{"text":"Woulfe, Patrick (1923). Irish Names and Surnames: \"Muire — Mary\". M. H. Gill & Son (Library Ireland edition).","url":"https://libraryireland.com/names/women/muire-mary.php"},{"text":"Behind the Name (Mike Campbell). \"Mary\": meaning, history and theories.","url":"https://www.behindthename.com/name/mary"},{"text":"Instituto Nacional de Estadística (May 2025). Nombres 2024: nombres más frecuentes de los residentes en España, población residente a 1 de enero de 2024 (infographic; archived copy, because the live URL now carries the 2025 edition).","url":"https://web.archive.org/web/20251129214225/https://ine.es/infografias/infografia_nombres.pdf"}]},{"id":"muhammad-mehmet-mamadou","icon":"🕌","tags":["given names","islam","turkish","spelling variants","naming statistics"],"title":{"en":"Muhammad, Mehmet, Mamadou","ja":"ムハンマド、メフメト、ママドゥ","ko":"무함마드, 메흐메트, 마마두","zh":"穆罕默德、梅赫梅特、马马杜","yue":"穆罕默德、梅赫梅特、馬馬杜","vi":"Muhammad, Mehmet, Mamadou","th":"มุฮัมมัด เมห์เมต มามาดู","id":"Muhammad, Mehmet, Mamadou","hi":"मुहम्मद, मेहमत, मामादू","de":"Muhammad, Mehmet, Mamadou","fr":"Muhammad, Mehmet, Mamadou","it":"Muhammad, Mehmet, Mamadou","es":"Muhammad, Mehmet, Mamadou","pt":"Muhammad, Mehmet, Mamadou","ru":"Мухаммад, Мехмет, Мамаду","uk":"Мухаммад, Мехмет, Мамаду","ar":"محمد، مَهمت، مامادو","he":"מוחמד, מהמט, ממדו","sw":"Muhammad, Mehmet, Mamadou"},"summary":{"en":"One Arabic name, محمد, became Mehmet in Turkish, Muhammadu in Hausa, Mamadou in West Africa and Magomed in Russian. Turkish keeps a separate form, Muhammed, for the Prophet. England and Wales count each spelling separately, and on those counts the single spelling Muhammad has been the top boys' name since 2023.","ja":"アラビア語の名 محمد は、トルコ語ではメフメト、ハウサ語ではムハンマドゥ、西アフリカではママドゥ、ロシア語ではマゴメドになった。トルコ語は預言者を指すときにはムハンメドという別の形を使う。イングランドとウェールズの統計は綴りごとに別々に数えるが、2023年以降はMuhammadという1つの綴りだけで男の子の名前の1位になっている。","ko":"아랍어 이름 محمد 하나가 튀르키예어에서는 Mehmet, 하우사어에서는 Muhammadu, 서아프리카에서는 Mamadou, 러시아어에서는 Magomed가 되었습니다. 튀르키예어는 예언자를 가리킬 때 Muhammed라는 별도의 형태를 씁니다. 잉글랜드와 웨일스는 철자마다 따로 집계하는데, 이 기준으로 Muhammad라는 단일 철자는 2023년부터 남자아이 이름 1위를 지키고 있습니다.","zh":"一个阿拉伯语名字 محمد，在土耳其语里成了 Mehmet，在豪萨语里成了 Muhammadu，在西非成了 Mamadou，在俄语里成了 Magomed。土耳其语另有一个专指先知的形式 Muhammed。英格兰和威尔士按拼写分别统计，按这一口径，单一拼写 Muhammad 自2023年起一直是男孩名第一。","yue":"一個阿拉伯文名 محمد，去到土耳其語變成 Mehmet，豪薩語變成 Muhammadu，西非變成 Mamadou，俄語就變成 Magomed。土耳其語另外留咗一個形式 Muhammed 專門指先知。英格蘭同威爾斯係將每個串法分開計，照呢個計法，單一串法 Muhammad 由2023年起一直係男仔名第一位。","vi":"Chỉ một tên tiếng Ả Rập, محمد, đã trở thành Mehmet trong tiếng Thổ Nhĩ Kỳ, Muhammadu trong tiếng Hausa, Mamadou ở Tây Phi và Magomed trong tiếng Nga. Tiếng Thổ Nhĩ Kỳ giữ riêng một dạng, Muhammed, để chỉ Nhà tiên tri. Anh và xứ Wales thống kê riêng từng cách viết, và theo cách đếm đó, riêng cách viết Muhammad đã là tên bé trai phổ biến nhất kể từ năm 2023.","th":"ชื่อภาษาอาหรับชื่อเดียว محمد กลายเป็น Mehmet ในภาษาตุรกี Muhammadu ในภาษาเฮาซา Mamadou ในแอฟริกาตะวันตก และ Magomed ในภาษารัสเซีย ภาษาตุรกียังเก็บรูป Muhammed ไว้ใช้เรียกท่านศาสดาโดยเฉพาะ อังกฤษและเวลส์นับแต่ละการสะกดแยกกัน และตามการนับนั้น การสะกดแบบ Muhammad เพียงแบบเดียวครองอันดับหนึ่งของชื่อเด็กชายมาตั้งแต่ปี 2023","id":"Satu nama Arab, محمد, menjadi Mehmet dalam bahasa Turki, Muhammadu dalam bahasa Hausa, Mamadou di Afrika Barat, dan Magomed dalam bahasa Rusia. Bahasa Turki menyimpan bentuk tersendiri, Muhammed, untuk Nabi. Inggris dan Wales menghitung setiap ejaan secara terpisah, dan menurut hitungan itu ejaan Muhammad saja sudah menjadi nama anak laki-laki teratas sejak 2023.","hi":"एक अरबी नाम, محمد, तुर्की में Mehmet, हाउसा में Muhammadu, पश्चिम अफ़्रीका में Mamadou और रूसी में Magomed बन गया। तुर्की में पैग़ंबर के लिए एक अलग रूप, Muhammed, रखा जाता है। इंग्लैंड और वेल्स हर वर्तनी को अलग-अलग गिनते हैं, और इस गिनती में अकेली वर्तनी Muhammad 2023 से लड़कों का सबसे लोकप्रिय नाम है।","de":"Aus einem einzigen arabischen Namen, محمد, wurde Mehmet im Türkischen, Muhammadu im Hausa, Mamadou in Westafrika und Magomed im Russischen. Für den Propheten hält das Türkische eine eigene Form bereit: Muhammed. England und Wales zählen jede Schreibweise einzeln, und nach dieser Zählung ist allein die Schreibung Muhammad seit 2023 der häufigste Jungenname.","fr":"Un seul nom arabe, محمد, est devenu Mehmet en turc, Muhammadu en haoussa, Mamadou en Afrique de l'Ouest et Magomed en russe. Le turc réserve une forme distincte, Muhammed, au Prophète. L'Angleterre et le pays de Galles comptent chaque graphie à part, et selon ce décompte, la seule graphie Muhammad est le premier prénom de garçon depuis 2023.","it":"Un unico nome arabo, محمد, è diventato Mehmet in turco, Muhammadu in hausa, Mamadou in Africa occidentale e Magomed in russo. Il turco riserva al Profeta una forma a parte, Muhammed. Inghilterra e Galles contano ogni grafia separatamente e, secondo questi conteggi, la sola grafia Muhammad è dal 2023 il nome maschile più diffuso.","es":"Un solo nombre árabe, محمد, se convirtió en Mehmet en turco, Muhammadu en hausa, Mamadou en África occidental y Magomed en ruso. El turco reserva una forma aparte, Muhammed, para el Profeta. Inglaterra y Gales cuentan cada grafía por separado y, según esas cifras, la grafía Muhammad por sí sola es el nombre de niño más común desde 2023.","pt":"Um único nome árabe, محمد, virou Mehmet em turco, Muhammadu em hauçá, Mamadou na África Ocidental e Magomed em russo. O turco reserva uma forma separada, Muhammed, para o Profeta. A Inglaterra e o País de Gales contam cada grafia à parte e, por essa contagem, a grafia Muhammad sozinha é o nome de menino mais comum desde 2023.","ru":"Одно арабское имя, محمد, стало Mehmet в турецком, Muhammadu в хауса, Mamadou в Западной Африке и Магомедом в русском. Для Пророка в турецком сохраняется отдельная форма — Muhammed. В Англии и Уэльсе каждое написание учитывается отдельно, и по этим подсчётам именно написание Muhammad с 2023 года — самое популярное имя для мальчиков.","uk":"Одне арабське ім'я, محمد, стало Mehmet у турецькій, Muhammadu в хауса, Mamadou в Західній Африці й Магомедом у російській. Для Пророка турецька зберігає окрему форму — Muhammed. В Англії та Уельсі кожне написання рахують окремо, і за цими підрахунками саме написання Muhammad від 2023 року є найпопулярнішим ім'ям для хлопчиків.","ar":"اسم عربي واحد، محمد، صار Mehmet في التركية، وMuhammadu في الهوسا، وMamadou في غرب أفريقيا، وMagomed في الروسية. وتحتفظ التركية بصيغة مستقلة للنبي هي Muhammed. وتُحصي إنجلترا وويلز كل تهجئة على حدة، وبهذا الإحصاء تتصدّر التهجئة الواحدة Muhammad أسماء الصبيان منذ عام 2023.","he":"שם ערבי אחד, محمد, הפך ל־Mehmet בטורקית, ל־Muhammadu בהאוסה, ל־Mamadou במערב אפריקה ול־Magomed ברוסית. הטורקית שומרת צורה נפרדת, Muhammed, לנביא. באנגליה ובוויילס סופרים כל כתיב בנפרד, ולפי ספירה זו הכתיב היחיד Muhammad הוא שם הבנים המוביל מאז 2023.","sw":"Jina moja la Kiarabu, محمد, liligeuka kuwa Mehmet katika Kituruki, Muhammadu katika Kihausa, Mamadou Afrika Magharibi na Magomed katika Kirusi. Kituruki kina umbo tofauti, Muhammed, kwa ajili ya Mtume. Uingereza na Wales huhesabu kila tahajia peke yake, na kwa hesabu hizo tahajia moja ya Muhammad imekuwa jina la kwanza la wavulana tangu 2023."},"body":{"en":"<p>The Arabic name <strong>محمد</strong> <em>Muḥammad</em> is the passive participle of <em>ḥammada</em> \"to praise greatly\", from the root ḥ-m-d that also gives <em>Aḥmad</em> and <em>Maḥmūd</em>: \"the praised one\". On the map the Arabic, Saudi, Persian and Urdu cells all carry the same four letters, محمد, and differ only in romanization, Arabic <em>Muḥammad</em> against Persian <em>Mohammad</em>. In Urdu it is commonly the first element before the name a man is called by: Pakistan's three-time prime minister Muhammad Nawaz Sharif is known as Nawaz Sharif. Outside the Arabic script the name was respelled to fit each language's sounds.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"muhammad\">👤 Show Muhammad on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"39\" data-lng=\"35\" data-zoom=\"4\">🌍 Pan to Turkey</button></div><h3>Mehmet and Muhammed</h3><p>The Turkish cell reads <strong>Mehmet</strong>. Ottoman Turkish wrote the name in Arabic script as the same محمد, and speakers read it <em>Mehemmed</em>, with Turkish <em>e</em> vowels, later shortened to <em>Mehmed</em>. The Latin alphabet adopted in 1928 spells it as pronounced, with a voiceless final <em>t</em>. Six Ottoman sultans were named Mehmed, among them Mehmed II, who took Constantinople in 1453. In 2025 Turkey's address-based population register counted 1,271,896 men named Mehmet, more than any other name, ahead of Mustafa (1,057,634) and Ahmet (857,105).</p><p>The Prophet himself is <em>Hz. Muhammed</em> in Turkish, never Mehmet. Some men are registered as <em>Muhammed</em> or <em>Muhammet</em>, but neither is among the register's ten most common men's names for 2025, a list that ends with İsmail at 434,992. The explanation usually given in Turkey is reverence: ordinary people received a modified form, and the exact name was kept for the Prophet. It is a traditional account, not a documented naming rule. Ottoman script wrote both identically as محمد, so whatever distinction existed lay in how the name was read aloud.</p><h3>West Africa and the Caucasus</h3><p>The Hausa cell shows <strong>Muhammadu</strong>, the name of Muhammadu Buhari, president of Nigeria from 2015 to 2023, with the everyday call-form <em>Mamman</em>. Hausa adds a final vowel to many Arabic loans that end in a consonant: <em>littafi</em> \"book\" from <em>al-kitāb</em>, <em>Lahadi</em> \"Sunday\" from <em>al-aḥad</em>. Further west, among Wolof, Fula and Manding speakers, the name is contracted to <strong>Mamadou</strong>, spelled the French way with <em>ou</em> for /u/. Mamadou Dia led Senegal's government from independence in 1960 until 1962; Mamadou Tandja was president of Niger from 1999 to 2010. Mamadou appears in the map's family tree but has no cell, because none of the 43 cells lies in Francophone West Africa.</p><p>The Russian cell shows <strong>Магомед</strong> <em>Magomed</em>, the usual Russian-language form in Dagestan and Chechnya. Russian has long written a foreign <em>h</em> with г, as in Гамбург for Hamburg, and older Russian texts call the Prophet Магомет, after the medieval European <em>Mahomet</em>. Bosnian <strong>Muhamed</strong>, on the Croatian cell, and French <strong>Mohamed</strong>, brought by North African families, both write a single <em>m</em>.</p><h3>Counting the spellings</h3><p>Muhammad is regularly called the most common name in the world, but no worldwide register of given names exists, and any such total depends on whether Mehmet, Mamadou and Magomed are counted as the same name. National statistics count spellings separately. The Office for National Statistics ranks names for England and Wales exactly as written on birth certificates; in 2015 that placed Muhammad 12th, Mohammed 29th and Mohammad 68th, and headlines calling Muhammad the top boys' name came from adding the spellings together.</p><p>Since 2023 the single spelling Muhammad has ranked first without that help. In 2025, 5,957 boys in England and Wales were registered as Muhammad, against 4,075 for Noah in second place. Another 1,712 were registered as Mohammed (20th), 895 as Mohammad (55th), 385 as Muhammed and 240 as Mohamed. In Wales taken alone, Muhammad ranked 34th.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"Office for National Statistics (2026). Baby names in England and Wales: 2025 — boys' names dataset (2025boysbabynames.xlsx: Tables 1, 3 and 6; Muhammad 5,957, Noah 4,075, Mohammed 1,712, Mohammad 895, Muhammed 385, Mohamed 240; Wales rank 34).","url":"https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/livebirths/datasets/babynamesenglandandwalesbabynamesstatisticsboys"},{"text":"Office for National Statistics (2016). The popularity of the name Muhammad/Mohammed/Mohammad (names ranked as written on birth certificates; 2015 ranks 12th, 29th, 68th).","url":"https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/livebirths/articles/thepopularityofthenamemuhammadmohammedmohammad/2016-09-02"},{"text":"Office for National Statistics (2024). Baby names in England and Wales: 2023 (Muhammad overtakes Noah as top boys' name; in the top 10 since 2016).","url":"https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/livebirths/bulletins/babynamesenglandandwales/2023"},{"text":"Habertürk / Anadolu Ajansı (2026). En yaygın isimler ve soyadları belli oldu (Adrese Dayalı Nüfus Kayıt Sistemi 2025: Mehmet 1,271,896; Mustafa 1,057,634; Ahmet 857,105; tenth, İsmail, 434,992).","url":"https://www.haberturk.com/turkiye-de-en-yaygin-isimlerde-mehmet-ve-fatma-soyadinda-yilmaz-zirvede-3883781"}]},{"id":"alexander-iskandar-sikandar","icon":"⚔️","tags":["names","arabic","persian","rebracketing","hungarian"],"title":{"en":"Iskandar and Sándor: Alexander Cut at Both Ends","ja":"イスカンダルとシャーンドル：前と後ろを削られたアレクサンドロス","ko":"이스칸다르와 샨도르: 앞뒤로 잘려 나간 알렉산드로스","zh":"伊斯坎德尔与山多尔：被两头截短的亚历山大","yue":"伊斯坎達爾同山多爾：一頭一尾被截短嘅亞歷山大","vi":"Iskandar và Sándor: Alexander bị cắt cả hai đầu","th":"อิสกันดาร์กับชานโดร์: อเล็กซานเดอร์ที่ถูกตัดทั้งหัวและท้าย","id":"Iskandar dan Sándor: Aleksander yang Terpotong di Kedua Ujungnya","hi":"इस्कंदर और शांडोर: दोनों सिरों से कटा अलेक्ज़ेंडर","de":"Iskandar und Sándor: Alexander, an beiden Enden gekürzt","fr":"Iskandar et Sándor : Alexandre, rogné aux deux bouts","it":"Iskandar e Sándor: Alessandro, tagliato da entrambe le parti","es":"Iskandar y Sándor: Alejandro, recortado por ambos extremos","pt":"Iskandar e Sándor: Alexandre cortado nas duas pontas","ru":"Искандар и Шандор: Александр, обрезанный с обоих концов","uk":"Іскандар і Шандор: Олександр, обрізаний з обох кінців","ar":"إسكندر وشاندور: الإسكندر مقصوصًا من طرفيه","he":"איסכנדר ושאנדור: אלכסנדר קטוע משני הקצוות","sw":"Iskandar na Sándor: Aleksanda Aliyekatwa Pande Zote Mbili"},"summary":{"en":"Arabic heard the Al- of Aléxandros as its own word for 'the' and dropped it, giving Iskandar, from which come Persian Eskandar and Urdu and Hindi Sikandar. Hungarian went the other way and kept only the back of the name, Sándor.","ja":"アラビア語はアレクサンドロスの頭の Al- を自分たちの定冠詞 al-「その」と聞き取って外し、イスカンダルが生まれた。ペルシア語のエスカンダル、ウルドゥー語・ヒンディー語のシカンダルはそこから来ている。ハンガリー語は逆に、名の後ろ半分だけを残してシャーンドルにした。","ko":"아랍어는 Aléxandros의 Al-을 자기 언어의 정관사 '그(the)'로 듣고 떼어 버려 Iskandar가 되었고, 여기서 페르시아어 Eskandar, 우르두어·힌디어 Sikandar가 나왔다. 헝가리어는 반대로 이름의 뒷부분만 남겨 Sándor가 되었다.","zh":"阿拉伯语把 Aléxandros 开头的 Al- 听成了自己的定冠词“the”，于是将其去掉，得到 Iskandar，波斯语的 Eskandar 和乌尔都语、印地语的 Sikandar 都由此而来。匈牙利语则反其道而行，只留下名字的后半截：Sándor。","yue":"阿拉伯語將 Aléxandros 開頭嘅 Al- 聽成自己個定冠詞「the」，就咁剷走咗，變成 Iskandar；波斯語嘅 Eskandar 同烏爾都語、印地語嘅 Sikandar 都係由呢度嚟。匈牙利語就反其道而行，淨係留低個名嘅後半截：Sándor。","vi":"Tiếng Ả Rập nghe phần Al- của Aléxandros như mạo từ xác định 'the' của chính mình và bỏ nó đi, tạo ra Iskandar; từ đó mà có Eskandar trong tiếng Ba Tư và Sikandar trong tiếng Urdu và tiếng Hindi. Tiếng Hungary thì làm ngược lại, chỉ giữ phần đuôi của cái tên: Sándor.","th":"ภาษาอาหรับได้ยิน Al- ใน Aléxandros เป็นคำนำหน้านามชี้เฉพาะ 'the' ของตนเอง จึงตัดทิ้งไป กลายเป็น Iskandar ซึ่งเป็นที่มาของ Eskandar ในภาษาเปอร์เซีย และ Sikandar ในภาษาอูรดูและภาษาฮินดี ส่วนภาษาฮังการีทำกลับกัน คือเก็บไว้แค่ท้ายชื่อ เป็น Sándor","id":"Bahasa Arab mendengar Al- pada Aléxandros sebagai kata sandangnya sendiri yang berarti 'si/sang', lalu membuangnya sehingga lahirlah Iskandar, asal Eskandar dalam bahasa Persia serta Sikandar dalam bahasa Urdu dan Hindi. Bahasa Hungaria justru sebaliknya dan hanya menyimpan bagian belakang nama itu: Sándor.","hi":"अरबी ने Aléxandros के Al- को अपना निश्चयवाचक उपपद 'अल' समझा और हटा दिया, जिससे Iskandar बना और उसी से फ़ारसी Eskandar तथा उर्दू और हिंदी Sikandar निकले। हंगेरियन ने उल्टी राह पकड़ी और नाम का केवल पिछला हिस्सा रखा: Sándor।","de":"Das Arabische hörte im Al- von Aléxandros seinen eigenen Artikel und ließ ihn weg. So entstand Iskandar, auf das persisch Eskandar sowie Urdu und Hindi Sikandar zurückgehen. Das Ungarische ging den umgekehrten Weg und behielt nur das Ende des Namens: Sándor.","fr":"L'arabe a entendu dans le Al- d'Aléxandros son propre mot pour « le » et l'a supprimé, d'où Iskandar, qui a donné le persan Eskandar et l'ourdou et l'hindi Sikandar. Le hongrois a fait l'inverse et n'a gardé que la fin du nom : Sándor.","it":"L'arabo sentì nell'Al- di Aléxandros la propria parola per «il» e la eliminò: nacque così Iskandar, da cui derivano il persiano Eskandar e l'urdu e l'hindi Sikandar. L'ungherese fece il contrario e tenne solo la parte finale del nome, Sándor.","es":"El árabe oyó en el Al- de Aléxandros su propia palabra para «el» y la suprimió. Así nació Iskandar, del que proceden el persa Eskandar y el urdu y el hindi Sikandar. El húngaro tomó el camino contrario y se quedó solo con la parte final del nombre: Sándor.","pt":"O árabe ouviu no Al- de Aléxandros a sua própria palavra para “o” e a eliminou, dando Iskandar, de onde vêm o persa Eskandar e o urdu e o hindi Sikandar. O húngaro fez o caminho inverso e ficou só com o final do nome: Sándor.","ru":"В Al- греческого Aléxandros арабский услышал собственный артикль и отбросил его. Так получился Iskandar, а от него — персидский Eskandar и Sikandar в урду и хинди. Венгерский пошёл обратным путём и оставил от имени только конец — Sándor.","uk":"В Al- грецького Aléxandros арабська почула власний артикль і відкинула його. Так з'явився Iskandar, а від нього — перський Eskandar і Sikandar в урду та гінді. Угорська пішла зворотним шляхом і залишила від імені тільки кінець — Sándor.","ar":"سمعت العربية مقطع Al- في Aléxandros كأنه أداة التعريف «الـ» فأسقطته، فكان Iskandar، ومنه جاء Eskandar الفارسي وSikandar في الأردية والهندية. أما المجرية فسلكت الاتجاه المعاكس ولم تحتفظ إلا بمؤخرة الاسم: Sándor.","he":"הערבית שמעה את ה-Al- שבראש Aléxandros כה' הידיעה שלה והשמיטה אותו, וכך נוצר Iskandar, וממנו Eskandar בפרסית ו-Sikandar באורדו ובהינדי. ההונגרית הלכה בכיוון ההפוך ושמרה רק את סוף השם: Sándor.","sw":"Kiarabu kilisikia Al- ya Aléxandros kama neno lake lenyewe la kibainishi na kukiondoa, na hivyo likazaliwa Iskandar, ambalo ndilo chanzo cha Eskandar wa Kiajemi na Sikandar wa Kiurdu na Kihindi. Kihungaria kilifanya kinyume chake na kubakiza sehemu ya mwisho tu ya jina: Sándor."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The missing first syllable</h3><p>On the map, the Greek cell reads <strong>Αλέξανδρος</strong> <em>Aléxandros</em>. Seven cells to the east have the same name without its first syllable: Arabic <strong>إسكندر</strong> <em>Iskandar</em>, Persian <strong>اسکندر</strong> <em>Eskandar</em>, Turkish <strong>İskender</strong>, Urdu <strong>سکندر</strong> and Hindi <strong>सिकंदर</strong> <em>Sikandar</em>, and Malay and Indonesian <strong>Iskandar</strong>.</p><p>The two languages that carried Alexander's story into the Near East both kept the syllable. The Syriac Alexander legend, written around 630, calls him <em>Aleksandrōs</em>. Middle Persian Zoroastrian writing, in the <em>Book of Ardā Wirāz</em>, calls him <em>gizistag Aleksandar ī Hrōmāyīg</em>, \"the accursed Alexander, the Roman\". The book says he killed the ruler of Iran, laid waste its capital and burned the Avesta. The syllable was lost in Arabic, where <em>Al-</em> sounds the same as the definite article <em>al-</em> \"the\". Speakers split the name into <em>al-</em> + <em>eksandar</em> and dropped the article, as they could from any noun. Two more changes followed. Greek <em>ks</em> (ξ) became <em>sk</em>, and because Arabic does not start a word with two consonants, the rest took a vowel in front: <em>Iskandar</em>. The city went the same way. Alexandria is <em>al-Iskandariyya</em> in Arabic, with the article back in place, this time as a real article.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"alexander\">👤 Show Alexander on the map</button></div><h3>Eskandar and Sikandar</h3><p>Persian took the Arabic form and ran into the same problem from the other side, because Persian also avoids <em>sk-</em> at the start of a word. It fixed it in two ways. <em>Eskandar</em> keeps the vowel in front. <em>Sekandar</em>, or <em>Sikandar</em> in classical and South Asian pronunciation, puts the vowel between the consonants. Ferdowsi's <em>Shāhnāme</em> (finished around 1010) makes Eskandar the son of the Persian king Dārāb and a daughter of Philip of Macedon, so the conqueror of Iran is half Iranian. In the late 12th century Nizami wrote a whole poem about him, the <em>Eskandar-nāme</em>.</p><p>Urdu and Hindi got <em>Sikandar</em> from Persian, which was the language of the Delhi sultanates and the Mughal court. Sultan Sikandar Lodi ruled Delhi from 1489 to 1517. Secunderabad, Hyderabad's twin city, is named after the Nizam Sikandar Jah (r. 1803–1829).</p><h3>The two-horned one</h3><p>Sura 18 of the Qur'an (verses 83–101) tells of <em>Dhū al-Qarnayn</em>, \"the one with two horns\". He travels to where the sun sets and to where it rises, and builds an iron barrier to keep out Gog and Magog. The name Alexander never appears in the text. Theodor Nöldeke in 1890, and Kevin van Bladel in 2008, compared the passage with the Syriac Alexander legend, in which Alexander makes the same journey and builds a gate against the same peoples. Van Bladel argues that the Qur'anic verses retell that legend, which was circulating in the last years of Muhammad's life. One proposed source for the horns is an image of Alexander himself. After he took Egypt in 332 BCE he was shown with the ram's horns of the god Ammon, and that image appears on coins struck by his successor Lysimachus.</p><p>Most classical commentators accepted that Dhū al-Qarnayn was Alexander, but not all of them. Ibn Kathir held that the Qur'anic figure was an earlier king and a believer, and that Alexander of Macedon, a polytheist, was a different man. Some early accounts named a Himyarite king of Yemen instead. In the 19th and 20th centuries, Sayyid Ahmad Khan and Abul Kalam Azad argued that he was Cyrus the Great. The link to Alexander comes from how the passage has been read; the text itself does not make it.</p><p>In the Malay world, the Greek name and the Qur'anic title are joined. The Malay Annals (<em>Sejarah Melayu</em>, whose oldest version is dated 1612) trace the kings of Melaka back to <em>Raja Iskandar Zulkarnain</em>, \"King Alexander the Two-Horned\". The <em>Hikayat Iskandar Zulkarnain</em> retells his life, probably working from an Arabic romance. Royal families kept using the name: Sultan Iskandar Muda ruled Aceh from 1607 to 1636. On the map, the Malay and Indonesian cells both read <strong>Iskandar</strong>.</p><h3>Sándor: the other end</h3><p>Hungarian cut the name from the other end. Ladó and Bíró's <em>Magyar utónévkönyv</em> (2005) describes <strong>Sándor</strong> as the Hungarian version of a shortened form of Alexander. It keeps the <em>-sander</em> part, the same piece of the name that the map lists as nicknames in Dutch (<em>Sander</em>) and Georgian (<em>Sandro</em>). In Hungarian spelling <em>s</em> stands for /ʃ/, so the name is pronounced [ˈʃaːndor], starting with the <em>sh</em> of English <em>shop</em>. The poet Sándor Petőfi (1823–1849) bore the name, and the everyday nickname is <em>Sanyi</em>.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"van Bladel, Kevin (2008). \"The Alexander Legend in the Qur'an 18:83–102.\" In G. S. Reynolds (ed.), The Qur'an in Its Historical Context, Routledge, pp. 175–203.","url":"https://www.academia.edu/33727330"},{"text":"Wiktionary (2025). إسكندر (ʔiskandar), Etymology: from Ancient Greek Ἀλέξανδρος, the initial syllable lost by rebracketing as the Arabic definite article al-.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%D8%A5%D8%B3%D9%83%D9%86%D8%AF%D8%B1"},{"text":"Wikipedia (2025). Dhu al-Qarnayn: Qur'an 18:83–101, classical identifications, Ṣaʿb Dhū Marāthid, the Cyrus theory (Sayyid Ahmad Khan, Abul Kalam Azad), Hikayat Iskandar Zulkarnain.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dhu_al-Qarnayn"},{"text":"Ladó, János & Bíró, Ágnes (2005). Magyar utónévkönyv. Vince Kiadó, Budapest, p. 109 (entry Sándor: 'az Alexander név rövidülésének magyaros alakja')."}]},{"id":"theophoric-names","icon":"✨","tags":["theophoric","hebrew","arabic","greek","etymology"],"title":{"en":"Names with God Inside","ja":"神が入っている名前","ko":"신이 들어 있는 이름","zh":"藏着神的名字","yue":"藏住神嘅名","vi":"Những cái tên chứa Thiên Chúa","th":"ชื่อที่มีพระเจ้าซ่อนอยู่","id":"Nama-Nama yang Menyimpan Tuhan","hi":"नाम, जिनमें ईश्वर बसा है","de":"Namen, in denen Gott steckt","fr":"Des prénoms qui renferment Dieu","it":"Nomi con Dio dentro","es":"Nombres con Dios dentro","pt":"Nomes com Deus dentro","ru":"Имена, в которых живёт Бог","uk":"Імена, у яких захований Бог","ar":"أسماء تحمل اسم الله","he":"שמות שהאל בתוכם","sw":"Majina Yenye Mungu Ndani Yake"},"summary":{"en":"Michael asks “Who is like God?”, Abdullah means “servant of God” and Theodore “gift of God”. This article shows where Hebrew -el and -yah, Arabic ʿabd and Greek theós sit inside common names, and what each language did to them.","ja":"ミカエルは「神に似た者が誰かいるだろうか」という問いで、アブドゥッラーは「神の僕」、テオドロスは「神の贈り物」を意味する。ヘブライ語の -el と -yah、アラビア語の ʿabd、ギリシャ語の theós が身近な名前のどこに入っているのか、そして各言語がそれをどう変えたのかを見ていく。","ko":"미카엘은 “누가 신과 같은가?”라고 묻고, 압둘라는 “신의 종”, 테오도르는 “신의 선물”이라는 뜻이다. 히브리어 -el과 -yah, 아랍어 ʿabd, 그리스어 theós가 흔한 이름 속 어디에 숨어 있는지, 그리고 각 언어가 이를 어떻게 바꾸어 놓았는지 살펴본다.","zh":"米迦勒在问“谁像神？”，阿卜杜拉意为“神的仆人”，西奥多意为“神的礼物”。本文带你看看希伯来语的 -el 和 -yah、阿拉伯语的 ʿabd、希腊语的 theós 藏在哪些常见名字里，以及各种语言又把它们变成了什么样。","yue":"米迦勒問「邊個似神？」，阿卜杜拉解「神嘅僕人」，狄奧多解「神嘅禮物」。呢篇文章話你知希伯來文嘅 -el 同 -yah、阿拉伯文嘅 ʿabd、希臘文嘅 theós 藏喺邊啲常見名入面，同埋每種語言將佢哋變成點。","vi":"Michael hỏi “Ai giống như Thiên Chúa?”, Abdullah nghĩa là “tôi tớ của Thượng đế” còn Theodore là “món quà của Thượng đế”. Bài viết chỉ ra -el và -yah của tiếng Hebrew, ʿabd của tiếng Ả Rập và theós của tiếng Hy Lạp nằm ở đâu trong những cái tên quen thuộc, và mỗi ngôn ngữ đã biến đổi chúng ra sao.","th":"มีคาเอลถามว่า “ใครเหมือนพระเจ้า?” อับดุลลาห์แปลว่า “บ่าวของพระเจ้า” และธีโอดอร์แปลว่า “ของประทานจากพระเจ้า” บทความนี้จะพาไปดูว่า -el และ -yah ของภาษาฮีบรู ʿabd ของภาษาอาหรับ และ theós ของภาษากรีก แฝงอยู่ตรงไหนในชื่อที่คุ้นเคย และแต่ละภาษาได้เปลี่ยนแปลงมันไปอย่างไร","id":"Mikhael bertanya “Siapa seperti Tuhan?”, Abdullah berarti “hamba Allah”, dan Theodorus “karunia Tuhan”. Artikel ini menunjukkan di mana unsur Ibrani -el dan -yah, Arab ʿabd, dan Yunani theós bersembunyi di dalam nama-nama umum, serta apa yang dilakukan tiap bahasa terhadapnya.","hi":"माइकल पूछता है “ईश्वर जैसा कौन?”, अब्दुल्लाह का अर्थ है “ईश्वर का दास” और थियोडोर का “ईश्वर का उपहार”। यह लेख दिखाता है कि हिब्रू -el और -yah, अरबी ʿabd और यूनानी theós आम नामों के भीतर कहाँ बैठे हैं, और हर भाषा ने उनके साथ क्या किया।","de":"Michael fragt „Wer ist wie Gott?“, Abdullah bedeutet „Diener Gottes“ und Theodor „Geschenk Gottes“. Dieser Artikel zeigt, wo hebräisches -el und -yah, arabisches ʿabd und griechisches theós in verbreiteten Namen stecken und was die einzelnen Sprachen daraus gemacht haben.","fr":"Michel demande « Qui est comme Dieu ? », Abdallah signifie « serviteur de Dieu » et Théodore « don de Dieu ». Cet article montre où se logent l’hébreu -el et -yah, l’arabe ʿabd et le grec theós dans des prénoms courants, et ce que chaque langue en a fait.","it":"Michele chiede «Chi è come Dio?», Abdullah significa «servo di Dio» e Teodoro «dono di Dio». Questo articolo mostra dove si nascondono l’ebraico -el e -yah, l’arabo ʿabd e il greco theós dentro nomi comuni, e che cosa ne ha fatto ciascuna lingua.","es":"Miguel pregunta «¿Quién como Dios?», Abdalá significa «siervo de Dios» y Teodoro, «don de Dios». Este artículo muestra dónde se esconden el hebreo -el y -yah, el árabe ʿabd y el griego theós dentro de nombres comunes, y qué hizo cada lengua con ellos.","pt":"Miguel pergunta “Quem é como Deus?”, Abdullah significa “servo de Deus” e Teodoro, “dádiva de Deus”. Este artigo mostra onde o hebraico -el e -yah, o árabe ʿabd e o grego theós se escondem em nomes comuns, e o que cada língua fez com eles.","ru":"Михаил спрашивает: «Кто как Бог?», Абдулла значит «раб Божий», а Феодор — «дар Божий». В статье показано, где в привычных именах прячутся еврейские -el и -yah, арабское ʿabd и греческое theós и что с ними сделал каждый язык.","uk":"Михаїл питає: «Хто як Бог?», Абдулла означає «раб Божий», а Теодор — «дар Божий». Стаття показує, де в поширених іменах ховаються єврейські -el і -yah, арабське ʿabd та грецьке theós і що з ними зробила кожна мова.","ar":"ميخائيل يسأل «مَن مثل الله؟»، وعبد الله معناه «عبدُ الله»، وثيودور معناه «عطية الله». يبيّن هذا المقال أين تقع العناصر العبرية -el و-yah، والعربية ʿabd، واليونانية theós داخل أسماء شائعة، وماذا فعلت كل لغة بها.","he":"מיכאל שואל “מי כאל?”, עבדאללה פירושו “עבד האל” ותאודור “מתת האל”. המאמר מראה היכן יושבים ה־-el וה־-yah העבריים, ה־ʿabd הערבי וה־theós היווני בתוך שמות נפוצים, ומה עשתה להם כל שפה.","sw":"Mikaeli anauliza “Ni nani aliye kama Mungu?”, Abdala maana yake ni “mja wa Mungu” na Theodori ni “zawadi ya Mungu”. Makala hii inaonyesha mahali ambapo -el na -yah za Kiebrania, ʿabd ya Kiarabu na theós ya Kigiriki zinapatikana ndani ya majina ya kawaida, na kile ambacho kila lugha ilizifanyia."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Name That Asks a Question</h3><p>The Hebrew name <strong>Mīḵāʼēl</strong> is a whole sentence: <em>mī</em> “who”, <em>kā</em> “like”, <em>ʼēl</em> “God”, so “Who is like God?”. A name built around a word for a god is called <strong>theophoric</strong>, and at least nine of the 50 names on this map are of that kind. The Hebrew Bible uses two such elements. One is <em>ʼēl</em>, the ordinary Semitic word for “god”. The other is a short form of the divine name YHWH, spelled <em>Yeho-</em> or <em>Yo-</em> at the start of a name and <em>-yah</em> or <em>-yāhū</em> at the end.</p><ul><li><strong>-el at the end:</strong> Michael; Gabriel, <em>Gaḇrīʼēl</em>, which the map glosses “God is my strength” (the first part is from the root g-b-r “to be strong”, also the source of <em>gever</em> “man”, so “man of God” is another reading); Daniel, <em>Dāniyyēl</em>, “God is my judge”.</li><li><strong>El- at the start:</strong> Elizabeth, <em>Elišévaʿ</em>, “my God is an oath”, the name of Aaron’s wife in Exodus 6:23.</li><li><strong>Yah:</strong> John, <em>Yōḥānān</em>, “Yahweh is gracious”; Matthew, <em>Mattityāhū</em>, “gift of Yahweh”.</li><li><strong>Both at once:</strong> Elijah, <em>ʼĒlīyāhū</em>: <em>ʼēlī</em> “my God” + <em>yāhū</em>, “my God is Yahweh”.</li></ul><p>Joseph, <em>Yōsēf</em> “he will add”, is a borderline case. It has no divine element as it stands, but Psalm 81:6 (81:5 in English Bibles) spells it <em>Yehōsēf</em>, with one. This suggests the short name is a clipped “may God add”.</p><h3>What the Languages Did to the God</h3><p>Once these names left Hebrew, the word for God meant nothing to the people using them. It became plain sound, and each language reshaped it:</p><ul><li><strong>Michael:</strong> Russian Михаил <em>Mikhail</em> keeps it as <em>-il</em>, Polish has Michał and Hungarian Mihály, and Romanian <em>Mihai</em> has lost the final <em>l</em>.</li><li><strong>Elizabeth:</strong> Spanish and Portuguese <em>Isabel</em> lost the opening <em>El-</em> (medieval Occitan had <em>Elisabel</em>), and Hungarian <em>Erzsébet</em> turned it into <em>Er-</em>.</li><li><strong>Elijah:</strong> <em>ʼēlī</em> became <em>Il-</em> and <em>-yāhū</em> shrank or vanished: Russian Илья <em>Ilya</em>, Romanian <em>Ilie</em>, Hungarian <em>Illés</em>.</li><li><strong>Matthew:</strong> Yahweh was lost before the name reached Greek, because Greek <em>Maththaîos</em> comes from the Aramaic short form <em>Mattay</em>. The map has Spanish <em>Mateo</em> and Russian Матвей <em>Matvei</em>.</li><li><strong>John:</strong> <em>Yō-</em> is reduced to a single opening sound in Dutch, Polish and Czech <em>Jan</em>, Russian Иван <em>Ivan</em> and Irish <em>Seán</em>.</li></ul><h3>Gift of God, in Greek</h3><p><strong>Theodore</strong> is Greek <em>Theódōros</em>, from <em>theós</em> “God” and <em>dōron</em> “gift”. It means the same as Matthew, and Dorothea has the same two parts in reverse order. The Slavs who received the name had no <em>th</em> sound and replaced Greek theta with <em>f</em>, which gives Russian Фёдор <em>Fyodor</em> and Ukrainian Федір <em>Fedir</em>. The map also has Hungarian <em>Tivadar</em>, Romanian <em>Tudor</em> and Amharic ቴዎድሮስ <em>Tewodros</em>, the name of Emperor Tewodros II of Ethiopia (r. 1855–1868).</p><h3>Servant of God, in Arabic</h3><p><strong>ʿAbd Allāh</strong> is <em>ʿabd</em> “servant” + <em>Allāh</em> “God”. Michael is opaque outside Hebrew, but Abdullah still breaks into two words that any Arabic speaker understands. Most Arabists derive <em>Allāh</em> from <em>al-ilāh</em> “the god”, and <em>ilāh</em> is related to Hebrew <em>ʼēl</em>; a minority look to Aramaic <em>alāhā</em>. The pattern is older than Islam. Muhammad’s ancestor ʿAbd Manāf was named for the deity Manāf, and Muhammad’s father was already called ʿAbd Allāh. In Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim (no. 2132), Ibn ʿUmar reports the Prophet naming ʿAbd Allāh and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān as the names dearest to God.</p><p>On the map, the Arabic cell writes it as two vowelled words, عَبْد اللّٰه, and the Saudi cell joins them as عبدالله. Elsewhere it appears as Persian <em>Abdollah</em>, Hausa <em>Abdullahi</em>, Swahili <em>Abdala</em>, French <em>Abdallah</em>, the Bosnian form <em>Abdulah</em> in the Croatian cell, and Chinese 阿卜杜拉 <em>Ābǔdùlā</em>.</p><p>The Sanskrit names on the map work differently. Krishna, Rama, Ganesh and Lakshmi are gods’ own names given directly to children, not sentences about a god.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"michael\">👤 Show Michael on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"abdullah\">👤 Show Abdullah on the map</button></div><h3>The Angels Keep Their -el</h3><p>Where the angels’ names came through Arabic, the old <em>ʼēl</em> is still there as <em>-īl</em>. The map shows Arabic ميكائيل <em>Mīkāʼīl</em> and جبريل <em>Jibrīl</em>, Turkish <em>Mikail</em> and <em>Cebrail</em>, and Persian میکائیل <em>Mikaʼil</em> and جبرئیل <em>Jabraʼil</em>.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"Joseph Jacobs, “Names (Personal)”, The Jewish Encyclopedia, vol. 9, Funk & Wagnalls, 1905","url":"https://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/11304-names-personal"},{"text":"Patrick Hanks, Kate Hardcastle & Flavia Hodges, A Dictionary of First Names, 2nd ed., Oxford University Press, 2006"},{"text":"Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj, Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim, Book of Manners, no. 2132 (Ibn ʿUmar: the names dearest to God are ʿAbd Allāh and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān), sunnah.com","url":"https://sunnah.com/muslim:2132a"},{"text":"Annemarie Schimmel, Islamic Names, Edinburgh University Press, 1989"}]},{"id":"nicholas-santa-claus","icon":"🎅","tags":["given-names","saints","christmas","etymology"],"title":{"en":"Nicholas, Sinterklaas, Santa Claus","ja":"ニコラオスからサンタクロースへ","ko":"니콜라스, 신터클라스, 산타클로스","zh":"尼古拉、辛特克拉斯与圣诞老人","yue":"尼古拉、辛特克拉斯同聖誕老人","vi":"Nicôla, Sinterklaas, Santa Claus","th":"นิโคลัส ซินเตอร์กลาส ซานตาคลอส","id":"Nikolas, Sinterklaas, Santa Claus","hi":"निकोलस, सिंटरक्लास, सांता क्लॉज़","de":"Nikolaus, Sinterklaas, Santa Claus","fr":"Nicolas, Sinterklaas, Santa Claus","it":"Nicola, Sinterklaas, Santa Claus","es":"Nicolás, Sinterklaas, Santa Claus","pt":"Nicolau, Sinterklaas, Santa Claus","ru":"Николай, Синтерклаас, Санта-Клаус","uk":"Миколай, Сінтерклаас, Санта-Клаус","ar":"نيكولاس، سينتركلاس، سانتا كلوز","he":"ניקולס, סינטרקלאס, סנטה קלאוס","sw":"Nikolao, Sinterklaas, Santa Claus"},"summary":{"en":"A Greek name meaning 'victory of the people' travelled with the cult of a 4th-century bishop, splitting into Nikolai, Mykola, Mikołaj and Miklós, wearing down to Klaus and Klaas, and reaching English in New York as Santa Claus.","ja":"「民の勝利」を意味するギリシャ語の名は、4世紀の司教への崇敬とともに広まった。各地でニコライ、ミコラ、ミコワイ、ミクローシュになり、短くなってクラウスやクラースになり、ニューヨークで英語のサンタクロースになった。","ko":"'민중의 승리'를 뜻하는 그리스어 이름이 4세기 한 주교에 대한 숭배와 함께 퍼지면서 니콜라이, 미콜라, 미코와이, 미클로시로 갈라지고, 클라우스와 클라스로 닳아 줄어든 끝에, 뉴욕에서 산타클로스라는 모습으로 영어에 들어왔다.","zh":"一个意为“人民的胜利”的希腊名字，随着对一位4世纪主教的崇拜四处流传，分化出Nikolai、Mykola、Mikołaj和Miklós，又磨损成Klaus和Klaas，最后在纽约以Santa Claus（圣诞老人）的形式进入英语。","yue":"一個解作「人民嘅勝利」嘅希臘名，跟住對一位4世紀主教嘅崇拜四圍傳開，分化成Nikolai、Mykola、Mikołaj同Miklós，又磨蝕成Klaus同Klaas，最後喺紐約以Santa Claus（聖誕老人）嘅形式進入英文。","vi":"Một cái tên Hy Lạp nghĩa là 'chiến thắng của dân chúng' đã lan đi theo việc tôn kính một giám mục thế kỷ 4, tách thành Nikolai, Mykola, Mikołaj và Miklós, mòn dần thành Klaus và Klaas, rồi đến với tiếng Anh tại New York dưới dạng Santa Claus.","th":"ชื่อกรีกที่แปลว่า 'ชัยชนะของประชาชน' เดินทางไปพร้อมกับการบูชาบิชอปแห่งศตวรรษที่ 4 แตกแขนงเป็น Nikolai, Mykola, Mikołaj และ Miklós สึกกร่อนลงเป็น Klaus และ Klaas แล้วเข้าสู่ภาษาอังกฤษที่นิวยอร์กในชื่อ Santa Claus","id":"Sebuah nama Yunani yang berarti “kemenangan rakyat” menyebar bersama penghormatan kepada seorang uskup abad ke-4, bercabang menjadi Nikolai, Mykola, Mikołaj, dan Miklós, terkikis menjadi Klaus dan Klaas, lalu masuk ke bahasa Inggris di New York sebagai Santa Claus.","hi":"'जनता की विजय' अर्थ वाला एक यूनानी नाम चौथी सदी के एक बिशप की उपासना के साथ फैला, निकोलाई, मिकोला, मिकोवाय और मिक्लोश में बँटा, घिसकर क्लाउस और क्लास बना, और न्यूयॉर्क में सांता क्लॉज़ बनकर अंग्रेज़ी तक पहुँचा।","de":"Ein griechischer Name mit der Bedeutung „Sieg des Volkes“ wanderte mit der Verehrung eines Bischofs aus dem 4. Jahrhundert, spaltete sich in Nikolai, Mykola, Mikołaj und Miklós auf, schliff sich zu Klaus und Klaas ab und gelangte in New York als Santa Claus ins Englische.","fr":"Un nom grec signifiant « victoire du peuple » a voyagé avec le culte d'un évêque du IVe siècle ; il s'est scindé en Nikolai, Mykola, Mikołaj et Miklós, s'est usé jusqu'à Klaus et Klaas, et il est entré dans l'anglais, à New York, sous la forme Santa Claus.","it":"Un nome greco che significa «vittoria del popolo» ha viaggiato con il culto di un vescovo del IV secolo, si è diviso in Nikolai, Mykola, Mikołaj e Miklós, si è consumato fino a Klaus e Klaas ed è arrivato nell'inglese, a New York, come Santa Claus.","es":"Un nombre griego que significa «victoria del pueblo» viajó con el culto a un obispo del siglo IV, se dividió en Nikolai, Mykola, Mikołaj y Miklós, se desgastó hasta quedar en Klaus y Klaas y llegó al inglés en Nueva York como Santa Claus.","pt":"Um nome grego que significa “vitória do povo” viajou com o culto a um bispo do século IV, dividiu-se em Nikolai, Mykola, Mikołaj e Miklós, desgastou-se até virar Klaus e Klaas e chegou ao inglês, em Nova York, como Santa Claus.","ru":"Греческое имя со значением «победа народа» распространилось вместе с почитанием епископа IV века, разошлось на формы Николай, Микола, Миколай и Миклош, сократилось до Клауса и Клааса и в Нью-Йорке пришло в английский как Санта-Клаус.","uk":"Грецьке ім'я, що означає «перемога народу», поширилося разом із шануванням єпископа IV століття, розійшлося на форми Ніколай, Микола, Міколай і Міклош, скоротилося до Клаус і Клаас і в Нью-Йорку прийшло в англійську як Санта-Клаус.","ar":"اسمٌ يونانيّ معناه «انتصار الشعب» انتقل مع تبجيل أسقفٍ من القرن الرابع، فتفرّع إلى نيكولاي وميكولا وميكواي وميكلوش، وتآكل حتى صار كلاوس وكلاس، ثم دخل الإنجليزية في نيويورك باسم سانتا كلوز.","he":"שם יווני שפירושו 'ניצחון העם' נדד עם פולחנו של בישוף מהמאה הרביעית, התפצל לניקולאי, מיקולה, מיקוואי ומיקלוש, נשחק לקלאוס ולקלאס, והגיע לאנגלית בניו יורק בדמות סנטה קלאוס.","sw":"Jina la Kigiriki lenye maana ya “ushindi wa watu” lilienea pamoja na ibada ya askofu wa karne ya 4, likagawanyika kuwa Nikolai, Mykola, Mikołaj na Miklós, likafupika hadi Klaus na Klaas, na likaingia katika Kiingereza huko New York kama Santa Claus."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Greek name carried by a saint</h3><p>Greek <strong>Νικόλαος</strong> (<em>Nikólaos</em>) joins <em>níkē</em> \"victory\" and <em>laós</em> \"people\". The name is older than its Christian fame: Nicolaus of Damascus wrote histories at the court of Herod in the 1st century BCE, and Acts 6:5 names a Nicolaus of Antioch among the seven deacons. Its spread across Europe, though, followed one man: Nicholas, bishop of Myra in Lycia, near modern Demre in Turkey's Antalya Province, whose traditional dates are 270–343. No writing by or about him survives from his lifetime. The earliest full <em>Life</em>, by Michael the Archimandrite, dates from the early 9th century, and it is the first source for the gift legend: gold thrown secretly through a window so that a poor man's three daughters would have dowries. In 1087 sailors from Bari carried most of his bones to southern Italy, where they arrived on 9 May.</p><h3>One saint, many local names</h3><p>Because the cult carried the name, the saint's December figure goes by the local form. On the map the Greek original Νικόλαος stands beside Latin-derived <strong>Nicolas</strong> (French), <strong>Nicolás</strong> (Spanish), <strong>Nicolau</strong> (Portuguese), <strong>Niccolò</strong> (Italian) and <strong>Nicolae</strong> (Romanian), with <strong>Nikola</strong> in Croatian, <strong>ნიკოლოზ</strong> (Nikoloz) in Georgian and <strong>Նիկոլ</strong> (Nikol) in Armenian. Further north and east the first consonant changes: Russian keeps <strong>Николай</strong> (Nikolai), but Ukrainian has <strong>Микола</strong> (Mykola), Polish <strong>Mikołaj</strong>, Czech <strong>Mikuláš</strong> and Hungarian <strong>Miklós</strong>. In Czech the name and the custom are one word: on the evening of 5 December, Mikuláš visits children with an angel and a devil. The Polish gift-bringer is Święty Mikołaj, \"Saint Nicholas\".</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"nicholas\">👤 Show Nicholas on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"50\" data-lng=\"15\" data-zoom=\"4\">🌍 Compare Nikolaus, Mikuláš and Miklós in Central Europe</button></div><h3>Short forms from the end, the front or the middle</h3><p>Many short forms drop the beginning of the name. German <strong>Nikolaus</strong> gives <em>Klaus</em> and Dutch <strong>Nicolaas</strong> gives <em>Klaas</em>; Danish <em>Niels</em> and Swedish <em>Nils</em> are medieval contractions of Latin <em>Nicolaus</em>, while the Swedish cell on the map shows the fuller <strong>Niklas</strong>. Other short forms keep the front instead: English <em>Nick</em>, Greek <em>Nikos</em>. Russian <em>Kolya</em> is built on the middle syllable. The Dutch saint, Sint Nicolaas, became <strong>Sinterklaas</strong> through the same kind of wear.</p><h3>From Sinterklaas to Santa Claus</h3><p>The English name is first recorded in New York. In December 1773 the <em>New-York Gazette and the Weekly Mercury</em> and <em>Rivington's New-York Gazetteer</em> reported that the anniversary of St. Nicholas, \"otherwise called St. A Claus\", had been celebrated at Protestant Hall. An 1808 number of <em>Salmagundi</em>, the periodical Washington Irving wrote with his brother William and James Kirke Paulding, mentions \"the noted St. Nicholas, vulgarly called Santaclaus\". Irving's 1809 <em>History of New York</em>, presented as the work of a Dutch historian, Diedrich Knickerbocker, makes St. Nicholas the patron of Dutch New Amsterdam. In December 1810 the New-York Historical Society, which John Pintard had founded in 1804, held its first St. Nicholas anniversary dinner, and Pintard paid for an engraved broadside of the saint.</p><p>Scholars disagree over who invented the New York figure. Charles W. Jones, in \"Knickerbocker Santa Claus\" (1954), found little trace of a St. Nicholas feast in the records of the Dutch colony and credited Irving's circle with creating the figure; that view became standard. Tom A. Jerman (2022) points out that the 1809 edition of Irving's <em>History</em> mentions St. Nicholas sixteen times only as a patron saint, never as Santa Claus, and that the gift-giving passages first appear in the 1812 edition. He dates the making of Santa Claus to 1821–1823 instead, and to the two texts below.</p><p>The reindeer first appear in 1821, in <em>The Children's Friend</em>, an eight-page booklet published in New York by William B. Gilley, where \"Santeclaus\" drives a single reindeer. On 23 December 1823 the <em>Troy Sentinel</em> printed \"Account of a Visit from St. Nicholas\" without an author's name. Clement Clarke Moore included it in his <em>Poems</em> of 1844; descendants of Henry Livingston Jr. disputed his authorship, and Don Foster made the case for Livingston in 2000. The poem calls its visitor St. Nicholas and St. Nick, never Santa Claus. Its last two reindeer were <em>Dunder</em> and <em>Blixem</em>, from Dutch <em>donder</em> \"thunder\" and <em>bliksem</em> \"lightning\"; later printings changed them to Donder and Blitzen.</p><h3>The red suit came before Coca-Cola</h3><p>Thomas Nast drew Santa Claus for <em>Harper's Weekly</em> from the issue of 3 January 1863 onward, and over about thirty years his Santa's coat changed from tan to red. Red-and-white Santas were on covers of <em>Puck</em> in the early 1900s. Haddon Sundblom's Santa for Coca-Cola first appeared in <em>The Saturday Evening Post</em> in 1931, and Sundblom painted his last version for the company in 1964. The company's own history page says that Santa wore red before Sundblom painted him. The ads fixed one version of the face and figure, but the colour was already there.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"Dave Wilton, Wordorigins.org, \"Santa Claus\" (Big List entry; citations from the New-York Gazette 1773, Salmagundi 25 January 1808, OED)","url":"https://www.wordorigins.org/big-list-entries/santa-claus"},{"text":"Charles W. Jones, \"Knickerbocker Santa Claus\", The New-York Historical Society Quarterly 38 (October 1954), reprinted by the St. Nicholas Center (on New Amsterdam records, Rivington's Gazetteer 1773, Pintard, the 1804 founding and the 1810 dinner)","url":"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/origin-of-santa/knickerbocker"},{"text":"Tom A. Jerman, \"Revisiting the 'Knickerbocker' Origin Story of Santa Claus\", History News Network, 18 December 2022 (on Charles W. Jones 1954, Irving 1809/1812, The Children's Friend 1821, Santa created 1821–1823)","url":"https://historynewsnetwork.org/article/revisiting-the-knickerbocker-origin-story-of-santa"},{"text":"The Coca-Cola Company, \"Haddon Sundblom and the Coca-Cola Santas\" (company history page: 1931 debut, final version 1964, red coat before Sundblom, Nast tan to red)","url":"https://www.coca-colacompany.com/about-us/history/haddon-sundblom-and-the-coca-cola-santas"}]},{"id":"george-yuri-yegor","icon":"🐉","tags":["saints","doublets","slavic","etymology","greek"],"title":{"en":"George, Yuri, Yegor: One Saint, Three Russian Names","ja":"ゲオルギー、ユーリー、エゴール——一人の聖人から生まれたロシアの三つの名","ko":"조지, 유리, 예고르: 한 성인, 세 개의 러시아 이름","zh":"乔治、尤里、叶戈尔：一位圣徒，三个俄语名字","yue":"佐治、尤里、葉戈爾：一個聖人，三個俄文名","vi":"George, Yuri, Yegor: Một vị thánh, ba cái tên Nga","th":"จอร์จ ยูริ เยกอร์: นักบุญองค์เดียว สามชื่อรัสเซีย","id":"George, Yuri, Yegor: Satu Santo, Tiga Nama Rusia","hi":"जॉर्ज, यूरी, येगोर: एक संत, तीन रूसी नाम","de":"Georg, Juri, Jegor: ein Heiliger, drei russische Namen","fr":"Georges, Iouri, Iegor : un saint, trois prénoms russes","it":"Giorgio, Jurij, Egor: un santo, tre nomi russi","es":"Jorge, Yuri, Yegor: un santo, tres nombres rusos","pt":"Jorge, Iuri, Iegor: um santo, três nomes russos","ru":"Георгий, Юрий, Егор: один святой и три русских имени","uk":"Георгій, Юрій, Єгор: один святий і три російські імені","ar":"جورج ويوري ويغور: قدّيس واحد وثلاثة أسماء روسية","he":"ג'ורג', יורי, יגור: קדוש אחד, שלושה שמות רוסיים","sw":"George, Yuri, Yegor: Mtakatifu Mmoja, Majina Matatu ya Kirusi"},"summary":{"en":"Greek Geōrgios means 'farmer'. Carried by the cult of a martyred soldier, it became Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö and Giorgi, and in Russian it split into three separate names: Georgy, Yuri and Yegor.","ja":"ギリシャ語のゲオルギオスは「農夫」という意味の名である。殉教した兵士への崇敬とともに広まってホルヘ、イジー、イェジ、ユルヨ、ギオルギとなり、ロシア語ではゲオルギー、ユーリー、エゴールという別々の三つの名に分かれた。","ko":"그리스어 Geōrgios는 '농부'라는 뜻이다. 순교한 병사를 기리는 숭배를 타고 이 이름은 Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö, Giorgi가 되었고, 러시아어에서는 게오르기, 유리, 예고르라는 세 개의 별개 이름으로 갈라졌다.","zh":"希腊语 Geōrgios 意为“农夫”。借着对一位殉道军人的崇拜，它变成了 Jorge、Jiří、Jerzy、Yrjö 和 Giorgi；在俄语里，它更分化成三个各自独立的名字：格奥尔基、尤里和叶戈尔。","yue":"希臘文 Geōrgios 解「農夫」。靠住對一位殉道軍人嘅崇拜，佢變成 Jorge、Jiří、Jerzy、Yrjö 同 Giorgi；喺俄文入面，仲分裂成三個獨立嘅名：格奧爾基、尤里同葉戈爾。","vi":"Tiếng Hy Lạp Geōrgios nghĩa là 'nông dân'. Theo chân việc tôn kính một người lính tử đạo, cái tên trở thành Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö và Giorgi, còn trong tiếng Nga nó tách thành ba tên riêng biệt: Georgy, Yuri và Yegor.","th":"ภาษากรีก Geōrgios แปลว่า 'ชาวนา' เมื่อแพร่ไปพร้อมกับลัทธิบูชาทหารผู้พลีชีพคนหนึ่ง ชื่อนี้จึงกลายเป็น Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö และ Giorgi ส่วนในภาษารัสเซียได้แตกออกเป็นสามชื่อที่แยกจากกัน คือ เกออร์กี ยูริ และเยกอร์","id":"Kata Yunani Geōrgios berarti “petani”. Terbawa oleh kultus seorang prajurit martir, nama ini menjadi Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö, dan Giorgi, dan dalam bahasa Rusia terpecah menjadi tiga nama terpisah: Georgy, Yuri, dan Yegor.","hi":"यूनानी Geōrgios का अर्थ है 'किसान'। एक शहीद सैनिक की पूजा के साथ यह नाम दूर-दूर तक फैला और Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö और Giorgi बना; रूसी में यह तीन अलग-अलग नामों में बँट गया: गेओर्गी, यूरी और येगोर।","de":"Griechisch Geōrgios bedeutet „Bauer“. Getragen vom Kult eines Soldatenmärtyrers wurde daraus Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö und Giorgi, und im Russischen spaltete er sich in drei eigenständige Namen: Georgi, Juri und Jegor.","fr":"Le grec Geōrgios signifie « agriculteur ». Porté par le culte d'un soldat martyr, il est devenu Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö et Giorgi, et en russe il s'est scindé en trois prénoms distincts : Gueorgui, Iouri et Iegor.","it":"Il greco Geōrgios significa «agricoltore». Diffuso dal culto di un soldato martire, è diventato Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö e Giorgi, e in russo si è diviso in tre nomi distinti: Georgij, Jurij ed Egor.","es":"El griego Geōrgios significa «agricultor». Difundido por el culto a un soldado mártir, dio Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö y Giorgi, y en ruso se dividió en tres nombres distintos: Gueorgui, Yuri y Yegor.","pt":"O grego Geōrgios significa “agricultor”. Levado pelo culto a um soldado mártir, tornou-se Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö e Giorgi, e em russo dividiu-se em três nomes distintos: Gueorgui, Iuri e Iegor.","ru":"Греческое Geōrgios значит «земледелец». Вместе с почитанием мученика-воина оно разошлось по миру и стало Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö и Giorgi, а в русском распалось на три отдельных имени: Георгий, Юрий и Егор.","uk":"Грецьке Geōrgios означає «хлібороб». Разом із шануванням мученика-воїна воно поширилося світом і стало Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö та Giorgi, а в російській розпалося на три окремі імені: Георгій, Юрій і Єгор.","ar":"الاسم اليوناني Geōrgios معناه «مزارع». حمله تبجيلُ جنديٍّ شهيد إلى أنحاء العالم، فصار Jorge وJiří وJerzy وYrjö وGiorgi، وانقسم في الروسية إلى ثلاثة أسماء مستقلة: غيورغي ويوري ويغور.","he":"השם היווני Geōrgios פירושו „איכר”. פולחנו של חייל שמת מות קדושים הפיץ אותו, והוא הפך ל־Jorge,‏ Jiří,‏ Jerzy,‏ Yrjö ו־Giorgi, וברוסית התפצל לשלושה שמות נפרדים: גאורגי, יורי ויגור.","sw":"Jina la Kigiriki Geōrgios lina maana ya “mkulima”. Likienezwa na ibada ya askari aliyeuawa kishahidi, likawa Jorge, Jiří, Jerzy, Yrjö na Giorgi, na katika Kirusi likagawanyika kuwa majina matatu tofauti: Georgy, Yuri na Yegor."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A farmer's name and a soldier's cult</h3><p>Greek <em>Geṓrgios</em> (Γεώργιος) is built on <em>geōrgós</em>, \"farmer\", from <em>gē</em> \"earth\" and <em>ergon</em> \"work\". Virgil's poem on farming, the <em>Georgics</em> (29 BCE), takes its title from the same word. The name spread because of one man: a soldier whose martyrdom tradition places at or near Lydda (Diospolis, now Lod in Israel), traditionally dated to 303 under Diocletian. The dragon came later. The earliest known version of that story is in an 11th-century Georgian source. It reached Latin Europe in the 12th century, and Jacobus de Voragine's <em>Golden Legend</em> of around 1260 fixed it for Western readers.</p><h3>Three names in Russian</h3><p>Russian has the name in three shapes and has kept all three. The vernacular Old East Slavic <em>Gjurgi</em> (Гюрги) became <strong>Юрий</strong> <em>Yuri</em>. A second popular form, <em>Yegory</em> (Егорий), dropped the initial <em>G-</em> and shortened to <strong>Егор</strong> <em>Yegor</em>. The church kept the Greek shape, <strong>Георгий</strong> <em>Georgy</em>. Max Vasmer's etymological dictionary of Russian traces all three to <em>Geōrgios</em>. Today they are separate names with separate diminutives, so the Russian cell on the map reads <em>Юрий / Георгий</em> and lists Yuri Gagarin beside Georgy Zhukov, two men named after the same saint. Ukrainian does the same with everyday <em>Юрій</em> <em>Yuriy</em> and learned <em>Heorhiy</em>.</p><p>Yuri was a princely name in Kievan Rus'. Yaroslav the Wise was baptised Yuri, and around 1030, according to the <em>Primary Chronicle</em>, he built a fort at what is now Tartu in Estonia and called it Yuryev. The Russian Empire revived that name for the city in 1893. Moscow first appears in the chronicles in 1147, as the place where Prince Yuri Dolgoruky received an ally.</p><p>The folk calendar used both forms of the name. The spring feast on 23 April (Julian) was <em>Yegory Veshny</em>, \"Spring Yegory\". The autumn feast on 26 November was Yuri's Day, and the law code of 1497 (the Sudebnik) made the week before and the week after it the only time of year when a peasant could leave one landowner for another. From the 1580s, decrees of \"forbidden years\" suspended that right, and the loss survives in the saying <em>Вот тебе, бабушка, и Юрьев день</em>, \"So much for Yuri's Day, granny\", said of a promise that has been broken.</p><h3>West and north</h3><p>Latin <em>Georgius</em> gave French <em>Georges</em>, Spanish and Portuguese <em>Jorge</em>, Italian <em>Giorgio</em> and German <em>Georg</em>, with Low German <em>Jürgen</em> beside it. Old Czech <em>Juří</em> became <strong>Jiří</strong>, the name of the 15th-century king Jiří z Poděbrad, and Old Polish borrowed it from Czech as <em>Jirzy</em> or <em>Jurzy</em>, which became <strong>Jerzy</strong>. Further east and south are Hungarian <em>György</em>, Croatian <em>Juraj</em> and Romanian <em>Gheorghe</em>. In the north, Swedish has <em>Göran</em> and <em>Örjan</em>, and Finnish <strong>Yrjö</strong> is recorded in medieval documents as <em>Yrjä</em> and <em>Yrjänä</em>. Dutch has <em>Joris</em>. In England the saint was patron from the 14th century, but the name itself was rare until the German-born George I took the throne in 1714. Five more British kings have carried it since.</p><h3>Georgia, Armenia, Ethiopia and the Arab world</h3><p>Georgian <strong>Giorgi</strong> (გიორგი) is the most common name in Georgia, according to the map's note. The country's name, though, is not taken from the saint. Georgians call their country <em>Sakartvelo</em>. The European <em>Georgia</em> most likely comes from the Persian name for the Georgians, <em>gurğ</em>, which Western crusaders and pilgrims met in the Levant in the 11th and 12th centuries; Russian <em>Gruziya</em> has the same source. Jacques de Vitry in the 13th century explained the name by the Georgians' devotion to St George, and others linked it to Greek <em>geōrgós</em>. Both are later explanations of a borrowed word.</p><p>Armenian has <em>Gevorg</em>, <em>Kevork</em> in Western Armenian. Amharic <em>Giyorgis</em> (ጊዮርጊስ) is the name of Ethiopia's patron saint and of the cross-shaped rock-cut church of Bete Giyorgis at Lalibela. Arabic-speaking Christians call the saint <em>Mār Jirjis</em> (جرجس), and also use <em>Jūrj</em>, taken from French or English.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"george\">👤 Show George on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"31.95\" data-lng=\"34.89\" data-zoom=\"6\">🌍 Go to Lod (Lydda), site of the martyrdom tradition</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"Wikipedia contributors (2026). Saint George (martyrdom at Lydda, 23 April 303; 11th-century Georgian dragon source; legend reaching Latin Europe in the 12th century; patronage of England from the 14th century; Ethiopia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_George"},{"text":"Wikipedia contributors (2026). Name of Georgia (country) (Persian gurğ, borrowing in the 11th or 12th century via crusaders and pilgrims in the Holy Land, St George and geōrgós folk etymologies, Jacques de Vitry)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Name_of_Georgia_(country)"},{"text":"Vasmer, M. (1964–1973). Etimologicheskiy slovar' russkogo yazyka, trans. O. N. Trubachyov, Moscow: Progress, s.v. Юрий, Георгий, Егор (as summarized in Wiktionary, 'Юрий')","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%D0%AE%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B9"},{"text":"Wikipedia contributors (2026). George's Day in Autumn (26 November O.S.; the week before and after as the only time peasants could move, after the Sudebnik of 1497; the saying 'so much for Yuri's Day, Granny')","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George%27s_Day_in_Autumn"}]},{"id":"elizabeth-isabel","icon":"👑","tags":["names","hebrew","royalty","etymology","europe"],"title":{"en":"Elizabeth and Isabel: One Name, Two Paths","ja":"エリザベスとイサベル：ひとつの名の二つの道","ko":"엘리자베스와 이사벨: 한 이름, 두 갈래 길","zh":"伊丽莎白与伊莎贝尔：同一个名字，两条道路","yue":"伊利沙伯同伊莎貝爾：一個名，兩條路","vi":"Elizabeth và Isabel: Một cái tên, hai ngả đường","th":"เอลิซาเบธกับอิซาเบล: ชื่อเดียว สองเส้นทาง","id":"Elizabeth dan Isabel: Satu Nama, Dua Jalan","hi":"एलिज़ाबेथ और इसाबेल: एक नाम, दो रास्ते","de":"Elisabeth und Isabel: ein Name, zwei Wege","fr":"Élisabeth et Isabelle : un nom, deux chemins","it":"Elisabetta e Isabella: un nome, due strade","es":"Elisabet e Isabel: un nombre, dos caminos","pt":"Elisabete e Isabel: um nome, dois caminhos","ru":"Елизавета и Изабелла: одно имя, два пути","uk":"Єлизавета та Ізабела: одне ім'я, два шляхи","ar":"إليزابيث وإيزابيل: اسم واحد وطريقان","he":"אליזבת ואיזבל: שם אחד, שתי דרכים","sw":"Elizabeth na Isabel: Jina Moja, Njia Mbili"},"summary":{"en":"Elizabeth and Isabel go back to one Hebrew name, Elisheba. Greek gave it a final -t and the Byzantine v. In southern France and Spain it lost its first syllable. Queens carried each form across Europe.","ja":"エリザベスとイサベルは、どちらもヘブライ語のエリシェバにさかのぼる。ギリシャ語で語末の -t とビザンツ式の v が加わり、南フランスとスペインでは最初の音節が落ちた。どちらの形も女王たちがヨーロッパ各地へ運んだ。","ko":"엘리자베스와 이사벨은 모두 히브리어 이름 엘리세바(Elisheba)로 거슬러 올라간다. 그리스어는 여기에 끝소리 -t와 비잔티움식 v를 더했다. 남프랑스와 스페인에서는 첫 음절이 떨어져 나갔다. 각 형태는 왕비들을 따라 유럽 곳곳으로 퍼졌다.","zh":"伊丽莎白和伊莎贝尔都源自同一个希伯来名字以利沙巴（Elisheba）。希腊语为它添上了词尾 -t 和拜占庭时期的 v 音。在法国南部和西班牙，它丢掉了第一个音节。王后们把各种形式带到了欧洲各地。","yue":"伊利沙伯同伊莎貝爾都源自同一個希伯來名以利沙巴（Elisheba）。希臘文幫佢加咗個詞尾 -t 同拜占庭嘅 v 音。喺法國南部同西班牙，佢甩咗第一個音節。各個形式就跟住王后去到歐洲各地。","vi":"Elizabeth và Isabel cùng bắt nguồn từ một tên tiếng Hebrew là Elisheba. Tiếng Hy Lạp thêm cho nó âm cuối -t và âm v thời Byzantine. Ở miền nam nước Pháp và Tây Ban Nha, tên này rụng mất âm tiết đầu. Các hoàng hậu, nữ vương đã mang từng dạng tên đi khắp châu Âu.","th":"เอลิซาเบธและอิซาเบลสืบย้อนไปถึงชื่อภาษาฮีบรูชื่อเดียวกัน คือเอลีเชบา (Elisheba) ภาษากรีกเติมเสียง -t ท้ายชื่อและเสียง v แบบไบแซนไทน์ให้ ในฝรั่งเศสตอนใต้และสเปน ชื่อนี้สูญพยางค์แรกไป และบรรดาราชินีก็พาชื่อแต่ละรูปไปทั่วยุโรป","id":"Elizabeth dan Isabel berasal dari satu nama Ibrani, Elisheba. Bahasa Yunani memberinya akhiran -t, dan Yunani Bizantium memberinya bunyi v. Di Prancis selatan dan Spanyol, nama ini kehilangan suku kata pertamanya. Para ratu membawa masing-masing bentuk ke seluruh Eropa.","hi":"एलिज़ाबेथ और इसाबेल दोनों एक ही हिब्रू नाम एलीशेबा से निकले हैं। यूनानी ने इसके अंत में -t जोड़ा और बीज़ान्टिनी यूनानी ने इसे v की ध्वनि दी। दक्षिणी फ़्रांस और स्पेन में इसका पहला अक्षर गिर गया। रानियाँ इसके हर रूप को पूरे यूरोप में ले गईं।","de":"Elisabeth und Isabel gehen auf einen einzigen hebräischen Namen zurück: Elisheba. Das Griechische gab ihm ein auslautendes -t, das byzantinische Griechisch das v. In Südfrankreich und Spanien verlor er seine erste Silbe. Königinnen trugen beide Formen durch ganz Europa.","fr":"Élisabeth et Isabelle remontent à un même nom hébreu, Elisheba. Le grec lui a donné un -t final, et le grec byzantin son v. Dans le sud de la France et en Espagne, il a perdu sa première syllabe. Des reines ont porté chacune des formes à travers l’Europe.","it":"Elisabetta e Isabella risalgono a un unico nome ebraico, Elisheba. Il greco gli diede una -t finale, il greco bizantino la v. Nella Francia meridionale e in Spagna perse la prima sillaba. Le regine portarono ciascuna forma in giro per l’Europa.","es":"Elisabet e Isabel se remontan a un mismo nombre hebreo, Elisheba. El griego le añadió una -t final, y el griego bizantino, la v. En el sur de Francia y en España perdió su primera sílaba. Las reinas llevaron cada forma por toda Europa.","pt":"Elisabete e Isabel remontam a um mesmo nome hebraico, Elisheba. O grego deu-lhe um -t final, e o grego bizantino, o v. No sul da França e na Espanha, perdeu a primeira sílaba. Rainhas levaram cada forma por toda a Europa.","ru":"Елизавета и Изабелла восходят к одному древнееврейскому имени — Элишева. Греческий добавил ему конечное -t, а Византия — звук v. На юге Франции и в Испании имя потеряло первый слог. Королевы разнесли каждую из форм по всей Европе.","uk":"Єлизавета та Ізабела походять від одного давньоєврейського імені — Елішева. Грецька додала йому кінцеве -t, а Візантія — звук v. На півдні Франції та в Іспанії ім'я втратило перший склад. Королеви рознесли кожну з форм по всій Європі.","ar":"يعود اسما إليزابيث وإيزابيل إلى اسم عبري واحد هو أليشابع. أضافت إليه اليونانية ‎-t في آخره، ومنحته البيزنطية صوت v. وفي جنوب فرنسا وإسبانيا سقط مقطعه الأول. وحملت الملكات كل صيغة منه عبر أوروبا.","he":"אליזבת ואיזבל חוזרות שתיהן לשם עברי אחד, אלישבע. היוונית הוסיפה לו ‎-t בסופו, והביזנטית את ה-v. בדרום צרפת ובספרד נשרה ההברה הראשונה שלו. מלכות נשאו כל אחת מהצורות ברחבי אירופה.","sw":"Elizabeth na Isabel zinatokana na jina moja la Kiebrania, Elisheba. Kigiriki kililipa -t ya mwisho, na Kigiriki cha Bizanti kikalipa sauti ya v. Kusini mwa Ufaransa na Hispania lilipoteza silabi yake ya kwanza. Malkia walibeba kila muundo kote Ulaya."},"body":{"en":"<h3>From Elisheba to Elisabet</h3><p>In Exodus 6:23 Aaron marries <strong>Elisheba</strong> (אֱלִישֶׁבַע, <em>Elišévaʿ</em>), daughter of Amminadab. The name joins <em>ʾēlî</em> \"my God\" and <em>šebaʿ</em> \"oath\", and is usually translated \"my God is an oath\". The Greek Septuagint wrote it <em>Elisabeth</em> (Ἐλισάβεθ). Greek had no <em>sh</em> sound, so <em>š</em> became <em>s</em>, the final Hebrew <em>ʿ</em> disappeared, and the name gained a final <em>-th</em> that the Hebrew does not have. Luke 1:5 gives the same name, spelled Ἐλισάβετ, to the mother of John the Baptist. Every Christian form on the map goes back to this New Testament Elisabet, and the Hebrew cell shows the original.</p><h3>The eastern and central forms</h3><p>By Byzantine times Greek <em>β</em> was pronounced [v], as it still is in Modern Greek Ελισάβετ (<em>Elisávet</em>). Church Slavonic took the name with that <em>v</em>: the Gospel of Luke has Елисавет, and the church calendar has Елисавета (<em>Yelisaveta</em>), with the feminine <em>-a</em>. Secular Russian has <em>z</em> in place of <em>s</em>: Елизавета (<em>Yelizaveta</em>), the form on the map. The best-known bearer is Empress Elizabeth Petrovna, who ruled Russia from 1741 to 1762. Armenian has Եղիսաբեթ (<em>Yeghisabet</em>).</p><p>Further west, three neighbouring languages cut the name down in similar ways. Polish <strong>Elżbieta</strong>, Czech <strong>Alžběta</strong> and Hungarian <strong>Erzsébet</strong> all drop the vowel after the first <em>l</em>, and all turn the <em>s</em> into a \"zh\" sound ([ʒ], in Polish [ʐ]), written <em>ż</em>, <em>ž</em> and <em>zs</em>. Hungarian also has <em>r</em> where the others have <em>l</em>. In this region the name was tied to Saint Elizabeth of Hungary (1207–1231), a daughter of King Andrew II who was canonised in 1235.</p><h3>Isabel: the Occitan detour</h3><p><strong>Isabel</strong> is usually called the Spanish form of Elizabeth. The <em>Dictionary of Medieval Names from European Sources</em> traces it to Old Provençal (Occitan) <em>Elisabel</em> instead, and its earliest records come from France: a Latin <em>Isabel</em> in the estate survey of Saint-Germain-des-Prés, c. 823, and <em>Ysabel</em> at Clairvaux in 1135. The first Iberian entry in the same dictionary is a Latin <em>Isabel</em> of 1487. That date reflects the dictionary's sampling, not first use: Isabel of Aragon, queen of Portugal, died in 1336. The usual explanation for the missing first syllable is that speakers heard <em>El-</em> as the Romance article <em>el</em> and dropped it. The change from <em>-beth</em> to <em>-bel</em> has no agreed explanation. One proposal points to Romance <em>bel</em>, <em>bella</em> \"beautiful\".</p><h3>Queens on both sides</h3><p>Royal marriages took the name to new countries. Isabella of Angoulême married King John of England in 1200, and Isabella of France married Edward II in 1308. The same dictionary has English records of Isabel from 1189 and of Elizabeth from 1279–80. The two were still known to be one name: in 16th-century England the same woman could be recorded as both, and into the 17th century Isabel was sometimes used as a pet form of Elizabeth. In the east, Zabel, the Armenian form of Isabel, was queen of Cilician Armenia from 1219 to 1252. Isabel I of Castile (1451–1504) became queen in 1474, and a second Isabel, Isabel II, reigned in Spain from 1833 to 1868.</p><p>In England, <strong>Elizabeth</strong> has been a popular name since the reign of Elizabeth I (1558–1603). The second queen of that name, Elizabeth II, reigned from 1952 to 2022.</p><h3>Sister names on the map</h3><p>Spain, Mexico and Portugal use <strong>Isabel</strong>, and Brazil uses <strong>Isabela</strong>. In other countries both forms are in use as separate names. France has <strong>Élisabeth</strong>, with Isabelle as its sister name. Italy has <strong>Elisabetta</strong> and Isabella, Romania has <strong>Elisabeta</strong> and Isabela, and the Philippines has <strong>Elizabeth</strong> and Isabel. Bible translations kept the older Greek-based shape. Korean 엘리사벳 (<em>Ellisabet</em>) comes from the Bible, while 엘리자베스 is a later borrowing from English. Arabic أليصابات (<em>Alīṣābāt</em>) is the name of Zechariah's wife in Arabic Bibles and a rare Christian given name.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"elizabeth\">👤 Show Elizabeth / Isabel on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"49\" data-lng=\"17\" data-zoom=\"5\">🌍 Pan to Elżbieta, Alžběta, Erzsébet</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"Uckelman, S.L. (ed.), A. Brown, J. Uckelman & S.L. Uckelman (2023). \"Isabel\" and \"Elizabeth\". The Dictionary of Medieval Names from European Sources, Edition 2023 no. 1.","url":"https://dmnes.org/name/Isabel"},{"text":"Campbell, Mike. \"Isabel\" and \"Elizabeth\". Behind the Name (online etymology of given names).","url":"https://www.behindthename.com/name/isabel"},{"text":"Hanks, Patrick, Kate Hardcastle & Flavia Hodges (2006). A Dictionary of First Names, 2nd ed. Oxford University Press (entries Elizabeth, Isabel).","url":"https://www.oxfordreference.com/display/10.1093/acref/9780198610601.001.0001/acref-9780198610601"},{"text":"\"Isabella, Queen of Armenia\" (Zabel, r. 1219–1252). Wikipedia.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella,_Queen_of_Armenia"}]},{"id":"rama-sita-southeast-asia","icon":"🏹","tags":["sanskrit","ramayana","thailand","royal-names","southeast-asia"],"title":{"en":"Rama and Sita in Southeast Asia: The Furrow, the Epic and the Numbered Kings","ja":"東南アジアのラーマとシーター――「畝」の名と叙事詩、番号で呼ばれる王たち","ko":"동남아시아의 라마와 시타: 밭고랑, 서사시, 그리고 번호가 붙은 왕들","zh":"东南亚的罗摩与悉多：犁沟、史诗与编号的国王","yue":"東南亞嘅羅摩同悉多：犁溝、史詩同編咗號嘅國王","vi":"Rama và Sita ở Đông Nam Á: Luống cày, sử thi và những vị vua được đánh số","th":"พระรามและนางสีดาในเอเชียตะวันออกเฉียงใต้: ร่องไถ มหากาพย์ และกษัตริย์ที่มีลำดับเลข","id":"Rama dan Sita di Asia Tenggara: Alur Bajak, Wiracarita, dan Raja-Raja Bernomor","hi":"दक्षिण-पूर्व एशिया में राम और सीता: हल-रेखा, महाकाव्य और क्रमांकित राजा","de":"Rama und Sita in Südostasien: die Furche, das Epos und die nummerierten Könige","fr":"Rama et Sita en Asie du Sud-Est : le sillon, l'épopée et les rois numérotés","it":"Rama e Sita nel Sud-est asiatico: il solco, l'epopea e i re numerati","es":"Rama y Sita en el Sudeste Asiático: el surco, la epopeya y los reyes numerados","pt":"Rama e Sita no Sudeste Asiático: o sulco, a epopeia e os reis numerados","ru":"Рама и Сита в Юго-Восточной Азии: борозда, эпос и пронумерованные короли","uk":"Рама і Сіта в Південно-Східній Азії: борозна, епос і пронумеровані королі","ar":"راما وسيتا في جنوب شرق آسيا: الأخدود والملحمة والملوك المرقَّمون","he":"ראמה וסיטה בדרום-מזרח אסיה: התלם, האפוס והמלכים הממוספרים","sw":"Rama na Sita katika Asia ya Kusini-Mashariki: Mtaro wa Jembe, Utenzi na Wafalme Wenye Namba"},"summary":{"en":"Sanskrit sītā means 'furrow' and rāma means either 'dark' or 'pleasing'. The Ramayana carried both names to Thailand, where Ayutthaya was named after Ayodhya. The Chakri kings have been numbered as Ramas only since Vajiravudh's reign (1910–1925).","ja":"サンスクリットのシーターは「畝」、ラーマは「黒い」または「快い」を意味する。この二つの名は『ラーマーヤナ』とともにタイへ渡り、アユタヤの都名はアヨーディヤーから採られた。ただし、チャクリー朝の王を「ラーマ○世」と番号で呼ぶのはワチラーウット王（在位1910～1925年）からである。","ko":"산스크리트어 sītā는 ‘밭고랑’을 뜻하고, rāma는 ‘검은’ 또는 ‘기쁨을 주는’을 뜻한다. 『라마야나』는 두 이름을 태국까지 전했고, 아유타야라는 이름은 아요디아에서 왔다. 짜끄리 왕조의 왕들을 라마 몇 세로 번호 매겨 부르게 된 것은 와치라웃 왕 치세(1910–1925)부터다.","zh":"梵语 sītā 意为‘犁沟’，rāma 则意为‘黑色的’或‘悦人的’。《罗摩衍那》把这两个名字带到了泰国，阿瑜陀耶便得名于阿约提亚。却克里王朝的国王以“拉玛”加序号相称，是从瓦栖拉兀在位时期（1910–1925）才开始的。","yue":"梵文 sītā 解‘犁溝’，rāma 就解‘黑色’或者‘悅人’。《羅摩衍那》將呢兩個名帶咗去泰國，阿瑜陀耶就係以阿約提亞命名。卻克里王朝嘅國王用「拉瑪」加序號嚟稱呼，係由瓦棲拉兀在位期間（1910–1925）先開始。","vi":"Trong tiếng Phạn, sītā nghĩa là ‘luống cày’, còn rāma nghĩa là ‘sẫm màu’ hoặc ‘dễ mến’. Sử thi Ramayana đã mang cả hai cái tên đến Thái Lan, nơi Ayutthaya được đặt tên theo Ayodhya. Các vua nhà Chakri chỉ được đánh số là Rama kể từ triều Vajiravudh (1910–1925).","th":"คำสันสกฤต sītā แปลว่า ‘ร่องไถ’ ส่วน rāma แปลได้ทั้ง ‘ดำคล้ำ’ หรือ ‘น่ารื่นรมย์’ รามายณะพาทั้งสองชื่อมาถึงประเทศไทย ซึ่งอยุธยาได้ชื่อตามอโยธยา การเรียกกษัตริย์ราชวงศ์จักรีเป็นพระรามลำดับที่ต่าง ๆ เพิ่งเริ่มขึ้นในรัชสมัยพระบาทสมเด็จพระมงกุฎเกล้าเจ้าอยู่หัว (วชิราวุธ, ค.ศ. 1910–1925)","id":"Dalam bahasa Sanskerta, sītā berarti “alur bajak”, sedangkan rāma berarti “gelap” atau “menyenangkan”. Ramayana membawa kedua nama itu ke Thailand, tempat Ayutthaya dinamai menurut Ayodhya. Raja-raja Chakri baru diberi nomor sebagai Rama sejak masa pemerintahan Vajiravudh (1910–1925).","hi":"संस्कृत में sītā का अर्थ है ‘हल-रेखा’ (कूँड़), और rāma का अर्थ या तो ‘साँवला’ है या ‘मनोहर’। रामायण दोनों नामों को थाईलैंड ले गई, जहाँ अयुत्थया का नाम अयोध्या पर रखा गया। चक्री वंश के राजाओं को ‘राम’ के रूप में क्रमांकित करने की प्रथा वजिरावुध के शासनकाल (1910–1925) से ही चली है।","de":"Sanskrit sītā bedeutet „Furche“, rāma entweder „dunkel“ oder „gefällig“. Das Ramayana brachte beide Namen nach Thailand, wo Ayutthaya nach Ayodhya benannt wurde. Als Ramas nummeriert werden die Chakri-Könige erst seit der Regierungszeit Vajiravudhs (1910–1925).","fr":"En sanskrit, sītā signifie « sillon » et rāma soit « sombre », soit « plaisant ». Le Ramayana a porté ces deux noms jusqu'en Thaïlande, où Ayutthaya doit son nom à Ayodhya. Les rois Chakri ne sont numérotés comme des Rama que depuis le règne de Vajiravudh (1910–1925).","it":"In sanscrito sītā significa «solco» e rāma «scuro» oppure «gradevole». Il Ramayana portò entrambi i nomi in Thailandia, dove Ayutthaya prese il nome da Ayodhya. I re Chakri vengono numerati come Rama solo dal regno di Vajiravudh (1910–1925).","es":"En sánscrito, sītā significa «surco» y rāma, «oscuro» o bien «agradable». El Ramayana llevó ambos nombres a Tailandia, donde Ayutthaya tomó su nombre de Ayodhya. A los reyes Chakri se los numera como Ramas solo desde el reinado de Vajiravudh (1910–1925).","pt":"Em sânscrito, sītā significa “sulco” e rāma, “escuro” ou “agradável”. O Ramayana levou os dois nomes à Tailândia, onde Ayutthaya recebeu o nome de Ayodhya. Os reis Chakri só são numerados como Ramas desde o reinado de Vajiravudh (1910–1925).","ru":"Санскритское sītā значит «борозда», а rāma — то ли «тёмный», то ли «приятный». «Рамаяна» принесла оба имени в Таиланд, где Аюттхая получила название в честь Айодхьи. Королей династии Чакри нумеруют как Рам лишь со времён правления Ваджиравудха (1910–1925).","uk":"Санскритське sītā означає «борозна», а rāma — або «темний», або «приємний». «Рамаяна» принесла обидва імені до Таїланду, де Аюттхаю назвали на честь Айодх'ї. Королів династії Чакрі нумерують як Рам лише від часів правління Ваджиравудха (1910–1925).","ar":"تعني الكلمة السنسكريتية sītā «الأخدود»، وتعني rāma إما «الداكن» وإما «المُبهِج». حملت ملحمة رامايانا الاسمين إلى تايلاند، حيث سُمّيت أيوتايا على اسم أيوديا. ولم يُرقَّم ملوك سلالة تشاكري باسم «راما» إلا منذ عهد فاجيرافوت (1910–1925).","he":"בסנסקריט sītā פירושו ‘תלם’, ו-rāma פירושו ‘כהה’ או ‘נעים’. הרמאיאנה הביאה את שני השמות לתאילנד, שבה נקראה איוטהאיה על שם איודהיה. מלכי שושלת צ'אקרי ממוספרים כ“ראמה” רק מאז ימי שלטונו של וג'ירבוד (1910–1925).","sw":"Katika Kisanskriti, sītā maana yake ni “mtaro wa jembe” na rāma maana yake ni “mweusi” au “anayependeza”. Ramayana ilipeleka majina yote mawili hadi Thailand, ambako Ayutthaya ilipewa jina kutokana na Ayodhya. Wafalme wa Chakri wamehesabiwa kwa namba kama Rama tangu enzi ya Vajiravudh (1910–1925) tu."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A furrow and a dark or pleasing one</h3><p>Sanskrit <em>sītā</em> is an ordinary noun: Monier-Williams' 1899 dictionary defines it as \"a furrow, the track or line of a ploughshare\". In Rigveda 4.57.6, Sītā is called on as the furrow that presides over the fruits of the earth. In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (12.69–72) she is addressed while four furrows are drawn in a ritual. The epic turns the noun into a story. King Janaka of Videha is ploughing ground for a sacrifice when he finds the infant in the furrow, and so she has the epithet <em>Ayonijā</em>, \"not born of a womb\".</p><p><em>Rāma</em> has two adjective senses, and the dictionary keeps them apart. In the Atharvaveda it means \"dark, dark-coloured, black\", and the dictionary compares <em>rātri</em> \"night\". From the Mahābhārata onward it means \"pleasing, pleasant, charming, lovely, beautiful\", from the root <em>ram-</em> \"to rest, to delight\". The name is older than the epic hero. Tradition credits Rigveda hymn 10.110 to a Rāma Jāmadagnya, and later Hinduism counts three Rāmas: Paraśurāma, Balarāma (\"the strong Rāma\") and Rāmacandra of the Ramayana. Because there are three of them, Sanskrit authors could also use <em>rāma</em> for the number three.</p><p>On the map, Hindi has राम <em>Rām</em> and सीता <em>Sītā</em>, and Bengali has রাম <em>Ram</em> and সীতা <em>Sita</em>. The Thai forms come with titles: พระราม <em>Phra Ram</em> takes the honorific <em>phra</em>, and นางสีดา <em>Nang Sida</em> takes <em>nang</em>, a title for a lady. The Indonesian cell has <em>Sinta</em>, the heroine's name in the Javanese wayang.</p><h3>The Ramakien</h3><p>Thailand's version of the epic is the <em>Ramakien</em>. Most of the Ayutthaya-period texts were lost when Burmese armies destroyed the city in 1767. The recension read today was compiled under King Rama I (r. 1782–1809), who wrote part of it himself. His son Rama II adapted it for <em>khon</em> masked drama, and the walls of Wat Phra Kaew in Bangkok are painted with its story. Phra Ram is an incarnation of Phra Narai (Vishnu), and Ravana becomes Thotsakan.</p><p>Sida's birth is different from the Sanskrit story. In the Ramakien she is born in Lanka as Thotsakan's own daughter. Astrologers foretell that she will bring misfortune on her father, so Thotsakan casts her out. The summary published by the Thailand Foundation goes on: a hermit buries her in the earth, entrusting her to Mother Earth, and sixteen years later unearths her and names her Sita, \"plough marks\". The hermit is Chanok (Janaka) himself, a king living as an ascetic. In the Thai telling he searches for the spot with a plough, finds her, and takes her home to Mithila as his daughter. The furrow is still where the girl is found, but now it gives back a child who was buried there, not one the earth produced.</p><h3>Ayutthaya and the Rama kings</h3><p>Ayutthaya's founding is conventionally dated to 1351. Its name is the Sanskrit <em>Ayodhyā</em>, Rama's capital. The word is built on <em>yudh</em> \"to fight\" and means \"not to be fought\", that is, invincible. The city's official name was Krung Thep Dvaravati Si Ayutthaya. Bangkok inherited the ceremonial name, and its long form still contains <em>Mahintharayutthaya</em>. Yogyakarta in Java is also named after Ayodhya. Royal titles used the name too. Ayutthaya's founder Uthong appears in the chronicles under the regnal name Ramathibodi I, and the Sukhothai king who ruled from 1279 to 1298 is known as Ram Khamhaeng, \"Rama the Bold\".</p><p>The numbered series Rama I to Rama X is much newer. It began with Vajiravudh (r. 1910–1925), who had been educated in England. Later in his reign he styled himself <em>Phra Ram thi Hok</em>, \"Rama the Sixth\". He is also credited with extending the style to every Chakri king back to 1782, following the European custom of numbering rulers who share a name. In Thai, a king is more usually identified by his reign: <em>Ratchakan thi Ha</em>, \"the Fifth Reign\", is Chulalongkorn. Calling Bhumibol (r. 1946–2016) \"Rama IX\" is mostly an English-language habit. The founder of the dynasty is known in Thai as Phra Phutthayotfa Chulalok, a name taken from a Buddha image that his grandson Rama III dedicated to him. He never heard himself called Rama I. But the name was already his: when he took the throne in 1782, his coronation title began <em>Phra Borommarachathirat Ramathibodi</em>.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"rama\">👤 Show Rama on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setname\" data-name=\"sita\">👤 Show Sita on the map</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"Monier-Williams, M. (1899). A Sanskrit-English Dictionary, entries rāma (p. 877) and sītā (p. 1218). Oxford: Clarendon Press; Cologne Digital Sanskrit Lexicon edition.","url":"https://www.sanskrit-lexicon.uni-koeln.de/scans/MWScan/2020/web/webtc/indexcaller.php"},{"text":"Wikipedia (accessed 2026). Rama (Kings of Thailand).","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rama_(Kings_of_Thailand)"},{"text":"Thailand Foundation (n.d.). Ramakien: Thailand's National Epic.","url":"https://thailandfoundation.or.th/ramakien-thailands-national-epic/"},{"text":"Wikipedia (accessed 2026). Ramakien.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramakien"}]}];
